  days: [
  { id: 'd1', name: 'DAY 1', range: '19·20번', story: '19 심경 변화 · 20 필자의 주장', passage: [['#', '19번 · 심경 변화', ''], ['', 'I ran into the music room, breathing heavily.', '나는 숨을 가쁘게 쉬며 음악실로 뛰어 들어갔다.'], ['', 'The rest of the band were sitting with long faces.', '밴드의 나머지 부원들은 시무룩한 얼굴로 앉아 있었다.'], ['', 'Immy looked up at me and shook her head.', 'Immy는 나를 올려다보며 고개를 가로저었다.'], ['', 'I sank into a chair.', '나는 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다.'], ['', 'I had truly believed we would make it; our audition had been flawless.', '나는 정말로 우리가 해낼 거라고 믿었었다. 우리의 오디션은 완벽했으니까.'], ['', 'My chest felt heavy as I asked, "So, who won?"', '"그래서, 누가 우승했어?"라고 물으며 나는 가슴이 무거웠다.'], ['', 'I was holding back my tears.', '나는 눈물을 참고 있었다.'], ['', '"A band called A Happy Accident. They were quite good," she said calmly.', '"A Happy Accident라는 밴드야. 꽤 잘하더라."라고 그녀가 차분하게 말했다.'], ['', 'A moment later, a big smile spread across Immy\'s face, and the others began to cheer.', '잠시 후, Immy의 얼굴에 환한 미소가 번졌고, 다른 부원들이 환호하기 시작했다.'], ['', '"Wait, what? That\'s us!" I said.', '"잠깐, 뭐라고? 그건 우리잖아!"라고 내가 말했다.'], ['', 'Immy laughed and said, "Since you were late, we decided to play a joke on you! We made it!"', 'Immy가 웃으며 말했다. "네가 늦어서 장난 좀 치기로 했지! 우리가 해냈어!"'], ['', 'I was filled with joy.', '나는 기쁨으로 가득 찼다.'], ['', 'We jumped up and hugged each other.', '우리는 벌떡 일어나 서로를 껴안았다.'], ['', 'My heart was racing with excitement.', '내 심장은 흥분으로 마구 뛰고 있었다.'], ['#', '20번 · 필자의 주장', ''], ['', 'Only when you\'re happy that you\'ve done the best you can for your story should you show it to someone else.', '여러분이 자신의 이야기를 위해 할 수 있는 최선을 다했다고 만족할 때에만 그것을 다른 사람에게 보여 주어야 한다.'], ['', 'You may well want to show it to your nearest and dearest, and that\'s fine, but in most cases you shouldn\'t look to their reading for any great benefit to your work.', '가장 가깝고 소중한 사람들에게 그것을 보여 주고 싶은 것은 당연하고 그건 괜찮지만, 대부분의 경우 작품에 큰 도움이 되기를 바라며 그들의 읽기에 기대서는 안 된다.'], ['', 'The most valuable feedback is likely to come from other sources: you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you.', '가장 가치 있는 피드백은 다른 곳에서 나올 가능성이 크다. 즉, 독자는 여러분과의 친밀함이 아니라 비판적 통찰력을 보고 골라야 한다.'], ['', 'Fellow writers are ideal, but don\'t imagine that any writer will do.', '동료 작가들이 이상적이지만, 아무 작가나 괜찮을 거라고 생각하지는 마라.'], ['', 'If you are part of a writers\' circle or class, you\'ll know whose writing you admire, and if you\'ve had feedback in the past you\'ll know whose opinions have been helpful — and whose have not.', '작가 모임이나 수업의 일원이라면 누구의 글을 동경하는지 알 것이고, 과거에 피드백을 받아 본 적이 있다면 누구의 의견이 도움이 되었는지, 그리고 누구의 의견이 그렇지 않았는지 알 것이다.']], steps: [
    { type: 'vocab', title: '핵심어휘·숙어', quizN: 15, items: [['breathe heavily', '숨을 가쁘게 쉬다', 19], ['the rest of', '~의 나머지', 19], ['long face', '시무룩한 얼굴', 19], ['look up at', '~을 올려다보다', 19], ['shake one\'s head', '고개를 가로젓다', 19], ['sink into', '~에 털썩 주저앉다 (sink-sank-sunk)', 19], ['make it', '해내다, 성공하다', 19], ['flawless', '흠 없는, 완벽한', 19], ['chest', '가슴', 19], ['hold back', '(감정을) 참다', 19], ['calmly', '침착하게, 차분하게', 19], ['spread across', '~에 번지다', 19], ['cheer', '환호하다', 19], ['play a joke on', '~에게 장난치다', 19], ['be filled with', '~으로 가득 차다', 19], ['race', '(심장이) 마구 뛰다', 19], ['may well', '~하는 것도 당연하다', 20], ['nearest and dearest', '가장 가깝고 소중한 사람들', 20], ['look to A for B', 'A에게 B를 기대하다', 20], ['benefit', '이득, 도움', 20], ['valuable', '가치 있는', 20], ['source', '출처, 원천', 20], ['critical', '비판적인', 20], ['insight', '통찰(력)', 20], ['closeness', '친밀함', 20], ['fellow', '동료의', 20], ['ideal', '이상적인', 20], ['will do', '충분하다, 괜찮다', 20], ['circle', '모임, 동아리', 20], ['admire', '동경하다, 감탄하다', 20], ['opinion', '의견', 20]] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '실전 문항', guide: '시험지 원문항이에요. 지문을 읽고 답을 고르세요.', items: [
      { q: '<b>19. 다음 글에 드러난 \'I\'의 심경 변화로 가장 적절한 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">I ran into the music room, breathing heavily. The rest of the band were sitting with long faces. Immy looked up at me and shook her head. I sank into a chair. I had truly believed we would make it; our audition had been flawless. My chest felt heavy as I asked, "So, who won?" I was holding back my tears. "A band called A Happy Accident. They were quite good," she said calmly. A moment later, a big smile spread across Immy\'s face, and the others began to cheer. "Wait, what? That\'s us!" I said. Immy laughed and said, "Since you were late, we decided to play a joke on you! We made it!" I was filled with joy. We jumped up and hugged each other. My heart was racing with excitement.</span>', opts: ['① disappointed → delighted', '② nervous → indifferent', '③ confident → confused', '④ bored → thrilled', '⑤ proud → embarrassed'], ex: '정답 ▶ ① disappointed(실망한) → delighted(아주 기쁜)\n이유: 처음엔 우승을 놓친 줄 알고 의자에 주저앉아 눈물을 참아요(실망). 장난이었고 사실은 우리가 해냈다는 걸 알고 기쁨으로 가득 차 서로 껴안아요(기쁨).\n오답 ▶ ② nervous → indifferent(무관심한): 마지막에 무관심하지 않고 흥분해요 / ③ confident → confused: 처음 \'자신감\'은 오디션 전 마음이고, 글의 처음 심경은 실망. 마지막도 혼란이 아니라 기쁨 / ④ bored(지루한): 지루한 장면이 없어요 / ⑤ proud → embarrassed(당황한): 장난을 당했지만 부끄러워하지 않고 기뻐해요.\n포인트 ▶ 심경 변화는 \'처음 감정 표현(sank into a chair, holding back my tears)\'과 \'마지막 감정 표현(filled with joy, racing with excitement)\'을 찾으세요.', per: [['실망한 → 아주 기쁜', ['I was holding back my tears', 'I was filled with joy'], '정답'], ['긴장한 → 무관심한', 'My heart was racing with excitement', '✘ 끝은 흥분'], ['자신감 있는 → 혼란스러운', 'I had truly believed we would make it', '✘ 매력 오답: 처음 장면의 감정은 실망'], ['지루한 → 아주 신난', '', '✘ 지루한 장면 없음'], ['자랑스러운 → 당황한', '', '✘ 당황이 아니라 기쁨']], n: 19 },
      { q: '<b>20. 다음 글에서 필자가 주장하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">Only when you\'re happy that you\'ve done the best you can for your story should you show it to someone else. You may well want to show it to your nearest and dearest, and that\'s fine, but in most cases you shouldn\'t look to their reading for any great benefit to your work. The most valuable feedback is likely to come from other sources: you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you. Fellow writers are ideal, but don\'t imagine that any writer will do. If you are part of a writers\' circle or class, you\'ll know whose writing you admire, and if you\'ve had feedback in the past you\'ll know whose opinions have been helpful — and whose have not.</span>', opts: ['⑤ 글에 대한 피드백은 비판적 통찰을 줄 수 있는 사람에게 받아야 한다.', '① 작품을 완성할 때까지 수시로 피드백을 받아야 한다.', '② 소수의 전문가보다 일반 대중이 선호하는 작품을 써야 한다.', '③ 동료 작가와 친밀한 관계를 유지하면서 정보를 교류해야 한다.', '④ 글을 읽을 때 작가의 의견을 비판적으로 판단하며 읽어야 한다.'], ex: '정답 ▶ ⑤\n이유: 핵심 문장은 \'you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you.\' 가까운 사람이 아니라 비판적 통찰력이 있는 사람에게 피드백을 받으라는 주장이에요.\n오답 ▶ ① \'최선을 다한 뒤에만\' 보여 주라고 했으니 \'수시로\'와 반대 / ② 일반 대중 선호 이야기 없음 / ③ \'친밀함\'이 아니라 \'통찰력\'이 기준 (매력 오답: Fellow writers만 보고 고르기 쉬움) / ④ 글을 \'읽는\' 법이 아니라 피드백 받을 \'독자를 고르는\' 법\n포인트 ▶ 주장 문제는 should, must, 명령문이 들어간 문장에 집중!', per: [['비판적 통찰을 줄 수 있는 사람에게 피드백', 'you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you', '정답'], ['완성할 때까지 수시로 피드백', 'Only when you\'re happy that you\'ve done the best you can', '✘ 최선을 다한 뒤에만'], ['일반 대중이 선호하는 작품', '', '✘ 언급 없음'], ['동료 작가와 친밀한 관계 유지', 'don\'t imagine that any writer will do', '✘ 친밀함이 기준 아님'], ['작가 의견을 비판적으로 읽기', '', '✘ 독자 고르기가 핵심']], n: 20 }
    ] },
    { type: 'pick', title: '어휘선택', guide: '문맥에 맞는 낱말을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'I had truly believed we would make it; our audition had been {flawless|flawed}.', ex: '정답 ▶ flawless (흠 없는, 완벽한)\n오답 ▶ flawed (결함이 있는): 우리가 \'해낼 거라고 믿었던\' 이유이므로 오디션이 \'완벽했다\'가 맞아요. 반대말 짝으로 외워 두세요.' },
      { s: 'I was holding {back|up} my tears.', ex: '정답 ▶ back\n이유: hold back (감정을) 참다 → 눈물을 참고 있었다\n오답 ▶ hold up 들어 올리다, 지연시키다' },
      { s: '"A band called A Happy Accident. They were quite good," she said {calmly|angrily}.', ex: '정답 ▶ calmly (차분하게)\n오답 ▶ angrily (화를 내며): Immy는 장난을 치는 중이라 일부러 \'태연하게\' 거짓말을 했어요. 바로 뒤에 웃음을 터뜨려요.' },
      { s: 'A moment later, a big smile spread across Immy\'s face, and the others began to {cheer|cry}.', ex: '정답 ▶ cheer (환호하다)\n오답 ▶ cry (울다): 환한 미소 → 다른 부원들도 \'환호\'하기 시작했어요. 장난이 끝나는 순간이에요.' },
      { s: 'My heart was racing with {excitement|exhaustion}.', ex: '정답 ▶ excitement (흥분)\n오답 ▶ exhaustion (탈진): 기쁨으로 껴안은 뒤 심장이 \'흥분으로\' 뛰어요.' },
      { s: 'You may well want to show it to your nearest and dearest, and that\'s fine, but in most cases you shouldn\'t look to their reading for any great {benefit|burden} to your work.', ex: '정답 ▶ benefit (이득, 도움)\n오답 ▶ burden (부담): 가까운 사람들의 읽기에서 작품에 큰 \'도움\'을 기대하지 말라는 뜻이에요.' },
      { s: 'The most {valuable|available} feedback is likely to come from other sources: you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you.', ex: '정답 ▶ valuable (가치 있는)\n오답 ▶ available (이용 가능한): \'가장 가치 있는\' 피드백은 다른 곳에서 나온다. 철자 혼동 주의!' },
      { s: 'The most valuable feedback is likely to come from other sources: you should choose readers for their {critical|casual} insight, not their closeness to you.', ex: '정답 ▶ critical (비판적인)\n오답 ▶ casual (가벼운, 격식 없는): 친밀함과 대비되는 기준 = \'비판적\' 통찰력' },
      { s: 'Fellow writers are {ideal|idle}, but don\'t imagine that any writer will do.', ex: '정답 ▶ ideal (이상적인)\n오답 ▶ idle (게으른, 놀고 있는): 동료 작가들은 \'이상적인\' 독자지만, 아무나 되는 건 아니다.' },
      { s: 'If you are part of a writers\' circle or class, you\'ll know whose writing you {admire|admit}, and if you\'ve had feedback in the past you\'ll know whose opinions have been helpful — and whose have not.', ex: '정답 ▶ admire (동경하다, 감탄하다)\n오답 ▶ admit (인정하다): 누구의 글을 \'동경하는지\' 알면 그 사람을 독자로 고를 수 있어요.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'tf', title: 'T/F 내용 확인', guide: '본문 내용과 일치하면 T, 다르면 F를 고르세요.', items: [['The narrator arrived at the music room out of breath.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: breathing heavily = 숨을 가쁘게 쉬며\n어휘 ▶ out of breath 숨이 찬', 19, 'I ran into the music room, breathing heavily'], ['The narrator had doubts about the audition before hearing the result.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 해낼 거라고 정말로 믿었어요(truly believed). 의심하지 않았어요.\n어휘 ▶ have doubts about ~에 의심을 품다', 19, 'I had truly believed we would make it'], ['Immy told the truth about the winner at first.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 처음엔 다른 밴드 이름을 대며 장난을 쳤어요. 진실은 나중에 밝혔어요.', 19, 'Since you were late, we decided to play a joke on you'], ['The band members played a trick on the narrator because the narrator was late.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: Since you were late(네가 늦어서) 장난을 치기로 했어요.\n어휘 ▶ play a trick on = play a joke on', 19, 'Since you were late, we decided to play a joke on you'], ['The writer advises sharing your story only after you\'ve done your best on it.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: 최선을 다했다고 만족할 때에만(Only when ~) 보여 주라고 했어요.', 20, 'Only when you\'re happy that you\'ve done the best you can'], ['The writer says showing your work to family and close friends is wrong.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: \'that\'s fine\'(그건 괜찮다)이라고 했어요. 다만 큰 도움을 기대하지 말라는 거예요.', 20, 'and that\'s fine'], ['Readers should be chosen based on their critical insight.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: you should choose readers for their critical insight\n어휘 ▶ based on ~에 근거하여', 20, 'you should choose readers for their critical insight'], ['Any fellow writer will give equally useful feedback.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: \'don\'t imagine that any writer will do\' = 아무 작가나 괜찮다고 생각하지 마라\n어휘 ▶ equally 똑같이', 20, 'don\'t imagine that any writer will do']] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '일치·불일치', guide: '선택지는 본문을 어려운 어휘로 바꿔 쓴 문장이에요. 풀고 나서 해설의 어휘를 꼭 확인하세요.', items: [
      { q: '글의 내용과 <u>일치하는</u> 것은?', opts: ['The band\'s audition turned out to be successful.', 'The narrator was the first to arrive at the music room.', 'Immy smiled as soon as the narrator came in.', 'A Happy Accident was the name of another band that won.', 'The narrator cried loudly in front of everyone.'], ex: '정답 ▶ The band\'s audition turned out to be successful.\n이유: "We made it!" 우리가 해냈다 = 오디션 성공\n어휘 ▶ turn out to be ~인 것으로 드러나다', per: [['밴드의 오디션은 성공한 것으로 드러났다.', 'We made it', '일치'], ['화자가 음악실에 가장 먼저 도착했다.', 'The rest of the band were sitting with long faces', '불일치 → 다른 부원들이 이미 앉아 있었음'], ['화자가 들어오자마자 Immy가 웃었다.', 'Immy looked up at me and shook her head', '불일치 → 고개를 가로저음'], ['A Happy Accident는 우승한 다른 밴드였다.', 'That\'s us', '불일치 → 우리 밴드 이름'], ['화자는 모두 앞에서 크게 울었다.', 'I was holding back my tears', '불일치 → 눈물을 참음']], n: 19, box: ['19번 · 심경 변화'] },
      { q: '글의 내용과 일치하지 <u>않는</u> 것은?', opts: ['Feedback from loved ones is usually the most valuable.', 'Writers should do their best before sharing their work.', 'It is natural to want to show your work to loved ones.', 'Not every writer is a good reader of your work.', 'Past feedback can help you choose your readers.'], ex: '정답 ▶ Feedback from loved ones is usually the most valuable.\n이유: 가장 가치 있는 피드백은 \'다른 곳(other sources)\'에서 나와요. 가까운 사람들에게 큰 도움을 기대하지 말라고 했어요.\n어휘 ▶ loved ones 사랑하는 사람들 = nearest and dearest', per: [['사랑하는 사람들의 피드백이 대개 가장 가치 있다.', 'The most valuable feedback is likely to come from other sources', '불일치'], ['작가는 작품을 보여 주기 전에 최선을 다해야 한다.', 'Only when you\'re happy that you\'ve done the best you can', '일치'], ['사랑하는 사람들에게 보여 주고 싶은 건 자연스럽다.', 'You may well want to show it to your nearest and dearest', '일치 (may well = 당연하다)'], ['모든 작가가 좋은 독자인 것은 아니다.', 'don\'t imagine that any writer will do', '일치'], ['과거의 피드백이 독자를 고르는 데 도움이 된다.', 'you\'ll know whose opinions have been helpful', '일치']], n: 20, box: ['20번 · 필자의 주장'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'pick', title: '어법선택', guide: '어법상 알맞은 것을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'I ran into the music room, {breathing|breathed} heavily.', ex: '정답 ▶ breathing\n이유: 콤마 뒤 분사구문. 주어 I가 숨을 \'쉬는\'(능동) 주체 → 현재분사 breathing (= as I breathed heavily)\n오답 ▶ breathed를 쓰면 동사가 두 개(ran, breathed)가 접속사 없이 이어져요.' },
      { s: 'The rest of the band {were|was} sitting with long faces.', ex: '정답 ▶ were\n이유: the rest of + 명사 → of 뒤 명사에 수를 맞춰요. 여기서 the band는 \'밴드 부원들\'(구성원)을 뜻해 복수 취급 → were\n포인트 ▶ band, family, team은 \'구성원\'을 뜻하면 복수 취급 가능' },
      { s: 'Immy looked up at me and {shook|shaking} her head.', ex: '정답 ▶ shook\n이유: looked up ~ and shook ~ → 주어 Immy의 동사 두 개가 and로 병렬. 과거형 shook (shake-shook-shaken)' },
      { s: 'My chest felt {heavy|heavily} as I asked, "So, who won?"', ex: '정답 ▶ heavy\n이유: 감각동사 feel + 형용사(보어) = ~하게 느껴지다. 가슴이 \'무겁게\' 느껴졌다\n오답 ▶ heavily(부사)는 보어 자리에 못 와요.' },
      { s: '"A band {called|calling} A Happy Accident. They were quite good," she said calmly.', ex: '정답 ▶ called\n이유: A band (which was) called ~ = ~라고 \'불리는\' 밴드 → 수동의 과거분사 called\n오답 ▶ calling은 \'부르는\'(능동)' },
      { s: 'Only when you\'re happy that you\'ve done the best you can for your story {should you show|you should show} it to someone else.', ex: '정답 ▶ should you show\n이유: Only + 부사(절)이 문장 맨 앞에 오면 주어와 (조)동사의 순서가 바뀌어요(도치). Only when ~ should you show ~\n포인트 ▶ Only then did I realize ~ / Only after ~ can you ~' },
      { s: 'You may well want to show it to your nearest and dearest, and that\'s fine, but in most cases you shouldn\'t look to their reading {for|to} any great benefit to your work.', ex: '정답 ▶ for\n이유: look to A for B = A에게서 B를 기대하다 (짝꿍 표현)' },
      { s: 'The most valuable feedback is likely {to come|coming} from other sources: you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you.', ex: '정답 ▶ to come\n이유: be likely to + 동사원형 = ~할 가능성이 크다' },
      { s: 'Fellow writers are ideal, but don\'t imagine {that|what} any writer will do.', ex: '정답 ▶ that\n이유: 뒤에 완전한 절(any writer will do)이 와서 imagine의 목적어가 되는 접속사 that\n오답 ▶ what 뒤에는 불완전한 절' },
      { s: 'If you are part of a writers\' circle or class, you\'ll know {whose|who} writing you admire, and if you\'ve had feedback in the past you\'ll know whose opinions have been helpful — and whose have not.', ex: '정답 ▶ whose\n이유: \'누구의 글\'인지 → 뒤의 명사 writing을 꾸미는 whose (의문사)\n오답 ▶ who는 명사 앞에서 꾸미지 못해요.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'verb', title: '동사변형', guide: '( ) 안의 동사를 알맞은 형태로 바꿔 쓰세요.', items: [
      { s: 'I ___ into a chair.', v: 'sink', a: ['sank'], ex: '정답 ▶ sank\n이유: 과거 이야기 → 과거형. sink - sank - sunk (불규칙)' },
      { s: 'I had truly ___ we would make it; our audition had been flawless.', v: 'believe', a: ['believed'], ex: '정답 ▶ believed\n이유: had + p.p. = 과거완료. 결과를 듣기 \'전에 이미\' 믿고 있던 상태' },
      { s: 'A moment later, a big smile ___ across Immy\'s face, and the others began to cheer.', v: 'spread', a: ['spread'], ex: '정답 ▶ spread\n이유: spread는 현재·과거·과거분사가 모두 spread! (began과 같은 과거)' },
      { s: 'We jumped up and ___ each other.', v: 'hug', a: ['hugged'], ex: '정답 ▶ hugged\n이유: jumped up and hugged → 과거 병렬. 단모음 + 단자음(hug) → g를 겹쳐 -ged' },
      { s: 'Only when you\'re happy that you\'ve ___ the best you can for your story should you show it to someone else.', v: 'do', a: ['done'], ex: '정답 ▶ done\n이유: you\'ve(you have) + p.p. → 현재완료. do - did - done' },
      { s: 'If you are part of a writers\' circle or class, you\'ll know whose writing you admire, and if you\'ve ___ feedback in the past you\'ll know whose opinions have been helpful — and whose have not.', v: 'have', a: ['had'], ex: '정답 ▶ had\n이유: have + p.p. → 현재완료 \'받아 본 적이 있다\'(경험). have - had - had' },
      { s: 'Fellow writers are ideal, but don\'t ___ that any writer will do.', v: 'imagine', a: ['imagine'], ex: '정답 ▶ imagine\n이유: Don\'t + 동사원형 = ~하지 마라 (부정 명령문)' }
    ] },
    { type: 'error', title: '오류찾기', guide: '어법상 틀린 곳을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'I [[was]] filled [[of]] joy. We jumped up and [[hugged]] each other.', w: 1, fix: 'with', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ② of → with\n이유: be filled with ~ = ~으로 가득 차다 (= be full of)\n나머지 ▶ ① was(수동 be) ✔ ③ hugged(과거 병렬) ✔' },
      { s: 'I had truly believed we would [[make]] it; our audition [[has been]] [[flawless]].', w: 1, fix: 'had been', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ② has been → had been\n이유: 결과를 듣기 전, 과거의 그 시점에 이미 완벽했던 것 → 과거완료 had been (앞의 had believed와 시제를 맞춰요)\n나머지 ▶ ① make it(해내다) ✔ ③ flawless(보어 형용사) ✔' },
      { s: 'Immy [[laughed]] and said, "Since you were late, we decided [[playing]] a joke [[on]] you! We made it!"', w: 1, fix: 'to play', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ② playing → to play\n이유: decide는 to부정사를 목적어로 써요. decide to ~ = ~하기로 결정하다\n나머지 ▶ ① laughed ✔ ③ play a joke on(~에게 장난치다) ✔' },
      { s: 'Only when you\'re happy that you\'ve done the best you can for your story [[you should show]] it to [[someone]] [[else]].', w: 0, fix: 'should you show', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ① you should show → should you show\n이유: Only when ~(Only + 부사절)이 앞에 나오면 도치 → 조동사 + 주어 + 동사\n나머지 ▶ ② someone ③ else(someone else = 다른 누군가) ✔' },
      { s: 'The most [[valuable]] feedback is likely to come from other sources: you should choose readers [[for]] their critical insight, not their [[close]] to you.', w: 2, fix: 'closeness', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ③ close → closeness\n이유: their critical insight(명사)와 대등하게 비교되는 자리 → 명사 closeness(친밀함). their 뒤에도 명사가 와야 해요.\n나머지 ▶ ① valuable(최상급 the most + 형용사) ✔ ② choose A for B(B를 보고 A를 고르다) ✔' },
      { s: 'If you are part of a writers\' circle or class, you\'ll know whose writing you admire, and if you\'ve had feedback in the past you\'ll know [[whose]] opinions [[has been]] helpful — and whose [[have]] not.', w: 1, fix: 'have been', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ② has been → have been\n이유: 주어는 opinions(복수) → have been\n나머지 ▶ ① whose + 명사 ✔ ③ whose (opinions) have not (been helpful) ✔' }
    ] },
    { type: 'scramble', title: '본문 스크램블', guide: '우리말에 맞게 단어를 눌러 문장을 완성하세요.', items: [
      { ko: 'Immy는 나를 올려다보며 고개를 가로저었다.', en: 'Immy looked up at me and shook her head.' },
      { ko: '나는 눈물을 참고 있었다.', en: 'I was holding back my tears.' },
      { ko: '우리는 벌떡 일어나 서로를 껴안았다.', en: 'We jumped up and hugged each other.' },
      { ko: '동료 작가들이 이상적이지만, 아무 작가나 괜찮을 거라고 생각하지는 마라.', en: 'Fellow writers are ideal, but don\'t imagine that any writer will do.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'order', title: '문장순서', guide: '문장을 본문 순서대로 눌러서 배열하세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['I ran into the music room, breathing heavily.', 'The rest of the band were sitting with long faces.', 'Immy looked up at me and shook her head.', 'I sank into a chair.'], flow: ['숨차게 음악실로 뛰어 들어감', '부원들은 시무룩하게 앉아 있음', 'Immy가 고개를 가로저음', '의자에 털썩 주저앉음'] },
      { lines: ['A moment later, a big smile spread across Immy\'s face, and the others began to cheer.', '"Wait, what? That\'s us!" I said.', 'Immy laughed and said, "Since you were late, we decided to play a joke on you! We made it!"', 'I was filled with joy.'], flow: ['Immy의 얼굴에 미소, 부원들 환호', '잠깐, 그건 우리잖아!', '늦어서 장난친 거야, 우리가 해냈어!', '기쁨으로 가득 참'] },
      { lines: ['Only when you\'re happy that you\'ve done the best you can for your story should you show it to someone else.', 'You may well want to show it to your nearest and dearest, and that\'s fine, but in most cases you shouldn\'t look to their reading for any great benefit to your work.', 'The most valuable feedback is likely to come from other sources: you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you.', 'Fellow writers are ideal, but don\'t imagine that any writer will do.'], flow: ['최선을 다했을 때만 보여 줘라', '가까운 사람에게 보여 줘도 되지만 큰 도움은 기대 말라', '가장 가치 있는 피드백은 비판적 통찰을 가진 독자에게서', '동료 작가가 이상적이지만 아무나는 안 됨'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'insert', title: '문장삽입', guide: '주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 알맞은 곳을 고르세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['"A band called A Happy Accident. They were quite good," she said calmly.', 'A moment later, a big smile spread across Immy\'s face, and the others began to cheer.', '"Wait, what? That\'s us!" I said.'], given: 1, flow: ['다른 밴드가 우승했다고 차분하게 말함', '잠시 후 미소가 번지고 다른 부원들이 환호', '잠깐, 뭐라고? 그건 우리잖아!'], ex: '정답 ▶ ①\n이유: 장난이 깨지는 순간(미소 + 환호)이 있어야 \'그건 우리잖아!\'라고 알아챌 수 있어요.' },
      { lines: ['I had truly believed we would make it; our audition had been flawless.', 'My chest felt heavy as I asked, "So, who won?"', 'I was holding back my tears.'], given: 1, flow: ['해낼 거라 믿었음, 오디션은 완벽했음', '가슴이 무거운 채로 누가 우승했냐고 물음', '눈물을 참고 있었음'], ex: '정답 ▶ ①\n이유: 완벽했다고 믿었기에 더 실망스러워 가슴이 무거웠고, 묻는 동안 눈물을 참아요. 감정이 점점 무거워지는 순서예요.' },
      { lines: ['You may well want to show it to your nearest and dearest, and that\'s fine, but in most cases you shouldn\'t look to their reading for any great benefit to your work.', 'The most valuable feedback is likely to come from other sources: you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you.', 'Fellow writers are ideal, but don\'t imagine that any writer will do.'], given: 1, flow: ['가까운 사람들에게는 큰 도움을 기대하지 마라', '가장 가치 있는 피드백은 다른 곳에서: 통찰력으로 독자를 골라라', '동료 작가가 이상적이지만 아무나는 안 됨'], ex: '정답 ▶ ①\n이유: \'가까운 사람들 말고 → 다른 곳(other sources)에서\' 피드백을 받으라는 대비가 이어지고, 그 \'다른 곳\'의 예가 동료 작가예요.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'odd', title: '어색한 문장', guide: '글의 흐름과 관계없는 문장을 고르세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['I sank into a chair.', 'I had truly believed we would make it; our audition had been flawless.', 'I was sure that we had performed poorly at the audition.', 'My chest felt heavy as I asked, "So, who won?"'], odd: 2, ex: '정답 ▶ ③\n이유: 바로 앞에서 \'오디션이 완벽했다\'고 믿었다고 했어요. \'형편없이 했다고 확신했다\'는 정반대예요.' },
      { lines: ['You may well want to show it to your nearest and dearest, and that\'s fine, but in most cases you shouldn\'t look to their reading for any great benefit to your work.', 'The most valuable feedback is likely to come from other sources: you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you.', 'Family members always give the most honest and expert advice.', 'Fellow writers are ideal, but don\'t imagine that any writer will do.'], odd: 2, ex: '정답 ▶ ③\n이유: 필자는 가까운 사람들에게 큰 도움을 기대하지 말라고 했어요. \'가족이 늘 가장 전문적인 조언을 준다\'는 반대 주장이에요.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'blank', title: '빈칸추론', guide: '빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'I had truly believed we would make it; our audition had been ____.', opts: ['flawless', 'terrible', 'canceled', 'boring'], ex: '정답 ▶ flawless\n이유: \'해낼 거라고 정말로 믿었다\'의 근거예요. 오디션이 \'완벽했으니까\' 믿었던 거예요.' },
      { s: 'Immy laughed and said, "Since you were late, we decided to play a ____ on you! We made it!"', opts: ['joke', 'song', 'card', 'part'], ex: '정답 ▶ joke\n이유: 다른 밴드가 우승했다고 거짓말한 것 = 장난. play a joke on ~ = ~에게 장난치다' },
      { s: 'The most valuable feedback is likely to come from other sources: you should choose readers for their ____, not their closeness to you.', opts: ['critical insight', 'warm friendship', 'family ties', 'free time'], ex: '정답 ▶ critical insight\n이유: \'not their closeness to you\'와 대비되는 기준이에요. 친밀함(friendship, family ties)이 아닌 \'비판적 통찰력\'' },
      { s: 'Only when you\'re happy that you\'ve done ____ you can for your story should you show it to someone else.', opts: ['the best', 'the least', 'nothing', 'the fastest'], ex: '정답 ▶ the best\n이유: do the best you can = 할 수 있는 최선을 다하다. 최선을 다한 뒤에만 보여 주라는 뜻' }
    ] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '핵심문법', guide: '알맞은 답을 고르세요.', items: [
      { n: 19, q: '밑줄 친 부분의 의미로 가장 알맞은 것은?<br><b>I had truly believed we would <u>make it</u>.</b>', opts: ['해내다, 성공하다', '그것을 만들다', '제시간에 도착하다', '참석하다'], ex: '정답 ▶ 해내다, 성공하다\n이유: 오디션 합격 이야기이므로 \'해내다\'. 뒤에서 "We made it!"(우리가 해냈어!)으로 다시 나와요.\n포인트 ▶ make it은 \'제시간에 도착하다\', \'참석하다\' 뜻도 있으니 문맥으로 고르세요.' },
      { n: 19, q: '밑줄 친 <b>That</b>이 가리키는 것은?<br><b>"A band called A Happy Accident." ... "Wait, what? <u>That</u>\'s us!"</b>', opts: ['A Happy Accident (우승한 밴드 이름)', 'Immy', 'the audition', 'the music room'], ex: '정답 ▶ A Happy Accident\n이유: Immy가 말한 우승 밴드 이름이 바로 \'우리\' 밴드였어요. 그래서 장난이었다는 게 드러나요.' },
      { n: 19, q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>"______ you were late, we decided to play a joke on you!"</b>', opts: ['Since', 'Although', 'Unless', 'Until'], ex: '정답 ▶ Since\n이유: since = ~이니까(이유). \'네가 늦었으니까\' 장난을 쳤다\n오답 ▶ Although(~이지만), Unless(~하지 않으면), Until(~할 때까지)' },
      { n: 20, q: '밑줄 친 부분의 뜻으로 알맞은 것은?<br><b>You <u>may well</u> want to show it to your nearest and dearest.</b>', opts: ['~하는 것도 당연하다', '~하는 편이 낫다', '~해야만 한다', '~할 리가 없다'], ex: '정답 ▶ ~하는 것도 당연하다\n이유: may well + 동사원형 = ~하는 것도 당연하다, 아마 ~일 것이다\n비교 ▶ may as well = ~하는 편이 낫다' },
      { n: 20, q: '밑줄 친 부분의 뜻으로 알맞은 것은?<br><b>Don\'t imagine that any writer <u>will do</u>.</b>', opts: ['충분하다, 괜찮다', '할 것이다', '일할 것이다', '해낼 것이다'], ex: '정답 ▶ 충분하다, 괜찮다\n이유: do가 자동사로 \'충분하다, 적합하다\'의 뜻. any writer will do = 아무 작가나 괜찮다' },
      { n: 20, q: '밑줄 친 <b>whose have not</b> 뒤에 생략된 말은?<br><b>you\'ll know whose opinions have been helpful — and <u>whose have not</u>.</b>', opts: ['been helpful', 'had feedback', 'admired', 'written'], ex: '정답 ▶ been helpful\n이유: and whose (opinions) have not (been helpful). 앞과 겹치는 말을 생략했어요.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '요약문 완성', guide: '지문을 읽고 요약문의 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말을 고르세요.', items: [
      { n: 19, box: ['19번 · 심경 변화'], q: '다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것은?<br><b>The narrator was (A) ______, believing the band had lost, but became (B) ______ after learning it was just a joke.</b>', opts: ['(A) heartbroken – (B) overjoyed', '(A) heartbroken – (B) furious', '(A) relieved – (B) overjoyed', '(A) relieved – (B) bored', '(A) excited – (B) ashamed'], ex: '정답 ▶ (A) heartbroken – (B) overjoyed\n(A) 진 줄 알고 눈물을 참음 → heartbroken(비통한)\n(B) 장난이었고 합격 → overjoyed(매우 기쁜)\n오답 ▶ relieved(안도한)는 처음 감정과 맞지 않고, furious(격노한)·ashamed(부끄러운)는 마지막 감정과 달라요.', ev: ['I was holding back my tears', 'I was filled with joy'] },
      { n: 20, box: ['20번 · 필자의 주장'], q: '다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것은?<br><b>When getting feedback on your story, choose readers for their (A) ______ rather than their (B) ______ to you.</b>', opts: ['(A) critical insight – (B) closeness', '(A) closeness – (B) critical insight', '(A) popularity – (B) closeness', '(A) critical insight – (B) distance', '(A) kindness – (B) similarity'], ex: '정답 ▶ (A) critical insight – (B) closeness\n이유: you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you → A rather than B = B가 아니라 A\n오답 ▶ 순서를 바꾼 ②는 정반대예요.', ev: ['you should choose readers for their critical insight, not their closeness to you'] }
    ] }
  ] },
  { id: 'd2', name: 'DAY 2', range: '21·22번', story: '21 함축 의미 · 22 글의 요지', passage: [['#', '21번 · 함축 의미', ''], ['', 'A meeting in a flexible-time culture like those found in South America, parts of Europe, Africa, and the Middle East is more like an evergreen tree.', '남미, 유럽 일부, 아프리카, 중동에서 볼 수 있는 것과 같은 유연한 시간 문화에서의 회의는 상록수에 더 가깝다.'], ['', 'An agenda with a meeting start time and a topic will probably be circulated before the meeting.', '회의 시작 시간과 주제가 담긴 의제가 아마 회의 전에 배부될 것이다.'], ['', 'This will serve as the trunk of the tree.', '이것이 나무의 줄기 역할을 할 것이다.'], ['', 'But there\'s no expectation that the meeting will progress in a linear manner.', '하지만 회의가 일직선으로 진행될 것이라는 기대는 없다.'], ['', 'What seemed like a priority last week when the agenda was crafted is not necessarily the priority right now — so discussion may branch off in a new direction.', '의제를 만들던 지난주에 우선순위처럼 보였던 것이 지금 반드시 우선순위인 것은 아니다. 그래서 논의는 새로운 방향으로 가지를 뻗을 수 있다.'], ['', 'Other branches may sprout as some members have urgent phone calls that take them in and out of the meeting.', '일부 구성원이 긴급한 전화로 회의를 들락날락하면서 또 다른 가지들이 싹틀 수도 있다.'], ['', 'Or sub-groups may form within the room to discuss timely subjects linked in some way to the main meeting trunk.', '또는 회의실 안에서 소그룹이 생겨 주요 회의 줄기와 어떤 식으로든 연결된 시의적절한 주제를 논의할 수도 있다.'], ['', 'In flexible-time cultures, it seems clear that the most productive meetings grow in unpredictable ways, and the effective manager is flexible and professional enough to capitalize on priorities and changing needs as they arise.', '유연한 시간 문화에서는 가장 생산적인 회의가 예측할 수 없는 방식으로 성장하고, 유능한 관리자는 우선순위와 변화하는 요구가 생겨날 때 그것을 활용할 만큼 충분히 유연하고 전문적이라는 것이 분명해 보인다.'], ['', 'Interruptions, agenda changes, and frequent shifts in direction are seen as natural and necessary.', '중단, 의제 변경, 잦은 방향 전환은 자연스럽고 필요한 것으로 여겨진다.'], ['#', '22번 · 글의 요지', ''], ['', 'When we are "always on" we are constantly ingesting content — reading alerts and texts, liking, checking emails, downloading, uploading, upgrading, updating, deleting, submitting responses, and posting.', '우리가 \'항상 접속해\' 있을 때 우리는 끊임없이 콘텐츠를 섭취한다. 알림과 문자 읽기, \'좋아요\' 누르기, 이메일 확인, 다운로드, 업로드, 업그레이드, 업데이트, 삭제, 답변 제출, 그리고 게시물 올리기 말이다.'], ['', 'If we do it while we are eating, our multi-tasking is overloading us.', '만약 우리가 음식을 먹는 동안 그것을 한다면, 우리의 멀티태스킹은 우리에게 과부하를 주고 있는 것이다.'], ['', 'As our will attempts to digest the virtual content its forces are diverted from the physical content, our food.', '우리의 의지가 가상 콘텐츠를 소화하려고 애쓰는 동안, 그 힘은 물리적 내용물인 우리의 음식으로부터 다른 데로 돌려진다.'], ['', 'It\'s easy to eat a sandwich in a minute while talking on the phone.', '전화 통화를 하면서 1분 만에 샌드위치를 먹는 것은 쉽다.'], ['', 'Some people are skilled at doing both at the same time.', '어떤 사람들은 두 가지를 동시에 하는 데 능숙하다.'], ['', 'Others write a half-hearted, shorthand text while they hurriedly eat their sandwich at the same time.', '다른 사람들은 샌드위치를 급하게 먹으면서 동시에 성의 없고 줄여 쓴 문자를 보낸다.'], ['', 'The result can be a misunderstood text with the need for further clarifying texts and the beginnings of nervous indigestion.', '그 결과는 추가로 설명하는 문자가 필요한 오해받는 메시지, 그리고 신경성 소화 불량의 시작일 수 있다.'], ['', 'Eat your sandwich. Taste it. Let it go down. Then text.', '샌드위치를 먹어라. 맛을 느껴라. 넘어가게 하라. 그러고 나서 문자를 보내라.']], steps: [
    { type: 'vocab', title: '핵심어휘·숙어', quizN: 15, items: [['flexible', '유연한', 21], ['evergreen tree', '상록수', 21], ['agenda', '의제, 안건', 21], ['circulate', '배부하다, 돌리다', 21], ['serve as', '~의 역할을 하다', 21], ['trunk', '(나무의) 줄기', 21], ['linear', '선형의, 일직선의', 21], ['priority', '우선순위', 21], ['craft', '공들여 만들다', 21], ['not necessarily', '반드시 ~인 것은 아닌', 21], ['branch off', '갈라지다, 가지를 뻗다', 21], ['sprout', '싹트다', 21], ['urgent', '긴급한', 21], ['timely', '시의적절한', 21], ['productive', '생산적인', 21], ['unpredictable', '예측할 수 없는', 21], ['capitalize on', '~을 활용하다', 21], ['arise', '생기다, 발생하다', 21], ['interruption', '중단, 방해', 21], ['shift', '전환, 변화', 21], ['constantly', '끊임없이', 22], ['ingest', '섭취하다', 22], ['alert', '알림', 22], ['submit', '제출하다', 22], ['response', '응답', 22], ['overload', '과부하를 주다', 22], ['will', '의지', 22], ['attempt to', '~하려고 시도하다', 22], ['digest', '소화하다', 22], ['virtual', '가상의', 22], ['divert', '(방향을) 돌리다, 전환하다', 22], ['physical', '물리적인, 실제의', 22], ['be skilled at', '~에 능숙하다', 22], ['half-hearted', '성의 없는', 22], ['shorthand', '약칭의, 줄여 쓴', 22], ['hurriedly', '서둘러, 급하게', 22], ['clarify', '명확하게 하다', 22], ['indigestion', '소화 불량', 22]] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '실전 문항', guide: '시험지 원문항이에요. 지문을 읽고 답을 고르세요.', items: [
      { q: '<b>21. 밑줄 친 <u>an evergreen tree</u>가 다음 글에서 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">A meeting in a flexible-time culture like those found in South America, parts of Europe, Africa, and the Middle East is more like <u>an evergreen tree</u>. An agenda with a meeting start time and a topic will probably be circulated before the meeting. This will serve as the trunk of the tree. But there\'s no expectation that the meeting will progress in a linear manner. What seemed like a priority last week when the agenda was crafted is not necessarily the priority right now — so discussion may branch off in a new direction. Other branches may sprout as some members have urgent phone calls that take them in and out of the meeting. Or sub-groups may form within the room to discuss timely subjects linked in some way to the main meeting trunk. In flexible-time cultures, it seems clear that the most productive meetings grow in unpredictable ways, and the effective manager is flexible and professional enough to capitalize on priorities and changing needs as they arise. Interruptions, agenda changes, and frequent shifts in direction are seen as natural and necessary.</span>', opts: ['② a fluid process that adapts to shifting focuses', '① a fixed discussion without any disagreement', '③ a strict communication system that meets its needs', '④ a long-lasting business relationship that remains stable', '⑤ a consistent tradition that resists any sudden modifications'], ex: '정답 ▶ ② a fluid process that adapts to shifting focuses (바뀌는 초점에 적응하는 유동적인 과정)\n이유: 의제가 \'줄기\'이고, 논의가 새 방향으로 \'가지를 뻗고\', 소그룹이 \'싹트듯\' 생겨요. 회의가 일직선으로 가지 않고 상황에 맞춰 자라나는 모습 = 유동적인 과정\n오답 ▶ ① 고정된 논의(fixed) ✘ / ③ 엄격한 체계(strict) ✘ / ④ \'오래가는(long-lasting)\' 상록수의 특징만 보고 고르는 매력 오답 — 사업 관계 이야기 아님 / ⑤ 변화를 거부하는(resists) ✘ — 오히려 변화가 자연스럽다고 함\n포인트 ▶ 함축 의미는 비유의 \'어떤 특징\'을 말하는지 글 전체에서 찾기. 여기선 \'늘 푸름\'이 아니라 \'줄기에서 가지가 예측 없이 뻗어 나감\'', per: [['바뀌는 초점에 적응하는 유동적 과정', ['so discussion may branch off in a new direction', 'the most productive meetings grow in unpredictable ways'], '정답'], ['이견 없는 고정된 논의', 'there\'s no expectation that the meeting will progress in a linear manner', '✘'], ['필요를 충족하는 엄격한 소통 체계', '', '✘ strict ↔ flexible'], ['안정적으로 유지되는 오래가는 사업 관계', '', '✘ 상록수의 표면적 특징에 낚인 오답'], ['갑작스러운 변경을 거부하는 일관된 전통', 'are seen as natural and necessary', '✘ 변화가 자연스러움']], n: 21 },
      { q: '<b>22. 다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">When we are "always on" we are constantly ingesting content — reading alerts and texts, liking, checking emails, downloading, uploading, upgrading, updating, deleting, submitting responses, and posting. If we do it while we are eating, our multi-tasking is overloading us. As our will attempts to digest the virtual content its forces are diverted from the physical content, our food. It\'s easy to eat a sandwich in a minute while talking on the phone. Some people are skilled at doing both at the same time. Others write a half-hearted, shorthand text while they hurriedly eat their sandwich at the same time. The result can be a misunderstood text with the need for further clarifying texts and the beginnings of nervous indigestion. Eat your sandwich. Taste it. Let it go down. Then text.<br><small>* ingest: 섭취하다</small></span>', opts: ['② 식사 중 온라인 활동을 삼가고 음식 섭취에 집중해야 한다.', '① 규칙적인 식사는 건강한 식습관 형성에 필수적이다.', '③ 소셜 미디어를 통한 적극적인 소통은 스트레스 해소에 유익하다.', '④ 이메일 작성 시 내용을 구체적이고 명확하게 쓰는 것이 중요하다.', '⑤ 온라인에서 얻은 식품 관련 정보를 맹신하지 않도록 주의해야 한다.'], ex: '정답 ▶ ②\n이유: 먹으면서 온라인 활동을 하면 과부하가 걸리고, 문자는 오해를 부르고 소화도 안 된다. 그래서 마지막에 \'샌드위치를 먹고, 맛보고, 넘기고, 그다음에 문자하라\'고 해요. = 식사 중에는 음식에 집중하라\n오답 ▶ ① \'규칙적인\' 식사 이야기는 없음 / ③ SNS가 유익하다는 건 반대 / ④ 문자가 오해받는다는 건 \'결과\'일 뿐, 명확하게 쓰라는 게 요지 아님 (매력 오답) / ⑤ 식품 정보 이야기 없음\n포인트 ▶ 요지는 명령문(Eat your sandwich. ... Then text.)에 담겨 있어요.', per: [['식사 중 온라인 활동을 삼가고 음식에 집중', 'Eat your sandwich. Taste it. Let it go down. Then text', '정답'], ['규칙적인 식사의 필요성', '', '✘ 언급 없음'], ['SNS 소통은 스트레스 해소에 유익', 'our multi-tasking is overloading us', '✘ 반대'], ['이메일은 구체적·명확하게', 'The result can be a misunderstood text', '✘ 결과일 뿐 요지 아님'], ['온라인 식품 정보 맹신 주의', '', '✘ 언급 없음']], n: 22 }
    ] },
    { type: 'pick', title: '어휘선택', guide: '문맥에 맞는 낱말을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'An agenda with a meeting start time and a topic will probably be {circulated|calculated} before the meeting.', ex: '정답 ▶ circulated (배부된)\n오답 ▶ calculated (계산된): 의제가 회의 전에 \'돌려진다/배부된다\'. 철자 혼동 주의!' },
      { s: 'But there\'s no expectation that the meeting will progress in a {linear|lateral} manner.', ex: '정답 ▶ linear (일직선의)\n오답 ▶ lateral (옆의, 측면의): 회의가 \'일직선으로\' 진행되리라는 기대는 없다 → 가지처럼 뻗어 나감' },
      { s: 'Other branches may sprout as some members have {urgent|urban} phone calls that take them in and out of the meeting.', ex: '정답 ▶ urgent (긴급한)\n오답 ▶ urban (도시의): \'긴급한\' 전화 때문에 회의를 들락날락해요.' },
      { s: 'In flexible-time cultures, it seems clear that the most productive meetings grow in unpredictable ways, and the effective manager is flexible and professional enough to {capitalize|criticize} on priorities and changing needs as they arise.', ex: '정답 ▶ capitalize (on ~을 활용하다)\n오답 ▶ criticize (비판하다): 유능한 관리자는 변화하는 요구를 \'활용\'할 만큼 유연하다.' },
      { s: 'Interruptions, agenda changes, and frequent shifts in direction are seen as natural and {necessary|unnecessary}.', ex: '정답 ▶ necessary (필요한)\n오답 ▶ unnecessary (불필요한): 유연한 시간 문화에서는 중단과 변화가 \'자연스럽고 필요한\' 것이에요.' },
      { s: 'If we do it while we are eating, our multi-tasking is {overloading|unloading} us.', ex: '정답 ▶ overloading (과부하를 주는)\n오답 ▶ unloading (짐을 내리는): 먹으면서 온라인 활동까지 하면 \'과부하\'가 걸려요.' },
      { s: 'As our will attempts to digest the {virtual|visual} content its forces are diverted from the physical content, our food.', ex: '정답 ▶ virtual (가상의)\n오답 ▶ visual (시각의): 온라인의 \'가상\' 콘텐츠 ↔ 실제(physical) 음식의 대비' },
      { s: 'As our will attempts to digest the virtual content its forces are {diverted|devoted} from the physical content, our food.', ex: '정답 ▶ diverted (다른 데로 돌려진)\n오답 ▶ devoted (바쳐진): 의지의 힘이 음식에서 \'멀어져\' 가상 콘텐츠로 가요. divert A from B = A를 B에서 딴 데로 돌리다' },
      { s: 'Others write a {half-hearted|whole-hearted}, shorthand text while they hurriedly eat their sandwich at the same time.', ex: '정답 ▶ half-hearted (성의 없는)\n오답 ▶ whole-hearted (진심 어린): 급하게 먹으면서 쓰는 문자라 \'성의 없는\' 문자예요. 그래서 오해를 받아요.' },
      { s: 'The result can be a misunderstood text with the need for further clarifying texts and the beginnings of nervous {indigestion|ingestion}.', ex: '정답 ▶ indigestion (소화 불량)\n오답 ▶ ingestion (섭취): 급하게 먹은 결과 = \'소화 불량\'의 시작' }
    ] },
    { type: 'tf', title: 'T/F 내용 확인', guide: '본문 내용과 일치하면 T, 다르면 F를 고르세요.', items: [['In flexible-time cultures, an agenda is usually shared before a meeting.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: 의제가 회의 전에 배부될(circulated) 것이라고 했어요.\n어휘 ▶ share = circulate', 21, 'will probably be circulated before the meeting'], ['Meetings in flexible-time cultures are expected to follow the agenda step by step.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 일직선(linear)으로 진행될 거라는 기대가 없어요.\n어휘 ▶ step by step 차근차근', 21, 'there\'s no expectation that the meeting will progress in a linear manner'], ['Small groups may form during a meeting to talk about related topics.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: 소그룹이 생겨 줄기와 연결된 주제를 논의할 수 있어요.', 21, 'sub-groups may form within the room'], ['Interruptions are regarded as a sign of poor management.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 중단은 자연스럽고 필요한 것으로 여겨져요.\n어휘 ▶ be regarded as = be seen as', 21, 'are seen as natural and necessary'], ['Being "always on" means continuously taking in digital content.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: 항상 접속해 있을 때 끊임없이 콘텐츠를 섭취한다고 했어요.\n어휘 ▶ take in = ingest', 22, 'we are constantly ingesting content'], ['Multitasking while eating helps us digest food better.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 먹으면서 멀티태스킹하면 과부하가 걸리고, 힘이 음식에서 멀어져요.', 22, 'our multi-tasking is overloading us'], ['Texting while eating can lead to misunderstandings.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: 결과로 오해받는 문자(a misunderstood text)가 생길 수 있어요.', 22, 'The result can be a misunderstood text'], ['The writer suggests texting first and eating afterward.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 먼저 먹고, 그다음에 문자하라(Then text)고 했어요. 순서가 반대예요.', 22, 'Eat your sandwich. Taste it. Let it go down. Then text']] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '일치·불일치', guide: '선택지는 본문을 어려운 어휘로 바꿔 쓴 문장이에요. 풀고 나서 해설의 어휘를 꼭 확인하세요.', items: [
      { q: '글의 내용과 일치하지 <u>않는</u> 것은?', opts: ['Members are not allowed to leave the meeting for phone calls.', 'The agenda functions like the trunk of a tree.', 'Priorities may change after the agenda is written.', 'Productive meetings develop in unexpected ways.', 'Good managers make use of needs as they come up.'], ex: '정답 ▶ Members are not allowed to leave the meeting for phone calls.\n이유: 긴급한 전화로 회의를 들락날락할(in and out of) 수 있다고 했어요.\n어휘 ▶ function like ~처럼 기능하다 · make use of = capitalize on · come up = arise', per: [['구성원은 전화 때문에 회의를 떠날 수 없다.', 'urgent phone calls that take them in and out of the meeting', '불일치'], ['의제는 나무의 줄기처럼 기능한다.', 'This will serve as the trunk of the tree', '일치'], ['의제가 작성된 뒤 우선순위가 바뀔 수 있다.', 'is not necessarily the priority right now', '일치'], ['생산적인 회의는 예상치 못한 방식으로 발전한다.', 'the most productive meetings grow in unpredictable ways', '일치'], ['좋은 관리자는 요구가 생길 때 그것을 활용한다.', 'capitalize on priorities and changing needs as they arise', '일치']], n: 21, box: ['21번 · 함축 의미'] },
      { q: '글의 내용과 일치하지 <u>않는</u> 것은?', opts: ['Everyone finds it hard to do two things at once.', 'You can finish a sandwich in a minute while on the phone.', 'Hasty texts may require additional messages to explain them.', 'Eating while multitasking may upset the stomach.', 'The writer recommends paying attention to the taste of food.'], ex: '정답 ▶ Everyone finds it hard to do two things at once.\n이유: 어떤 사람들은 두 가지를 동시에 하는 데 능숙하다(Some people are skilled at doing both)고 했어요.\n어휘 ▶ hasty 서두른 · upset the stomach 배탈을 일으키다', per: [['모든 사람이 두 가지를 동시에 하기 어려워한다.', 'Some people are skilled at doing both at the same time', '불일치'], ['통화하면서 1분 만에 샌드위치를 먹을 수 있다.', 'It\'s easy to eat a sandwich in a minute while talking on the phone', '일치'], ['서둘러 쓴 문자는 설명하는 추가 문자가 필요할 수 있다.', 'with the need for further clarifying texts', '일치'], ['멀티태스킹하며 먹으면 배탈이 날 수 있다.', 'the beginnings of nervous indigestion', '일치'], ['필자는 음식의 맛에 집중하라고 권한다.', 'Eat your sandwich. Taste it', '일치']], n: 22, box: ['22번 · 글의 요지'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'pick', title: '어법선택', guide: '어법상 알맞은 것을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'A meeting in a flexible-time culture like {those|that} found in South America, parts of Europe, Africa, and the Middle East is more like an evergreen tree.', ex: '정답 ▶ those\n이유: 앞의 명사(cultures)를 대신하는 지시대명사. 남미, 유럽 등 여러 지역의 \'문화들\' → 복수 those\n포인트 ▶ those (which are) found in ~ = ~에서 발견되는 것들' },
      { s: 'A meeting in a flexible-time culture like those found in South America, parts of Europe, Africa, and the Middle East {is|are} more like an evergreen tree.', ex: '정답 ▶ is\n이유: 주어는 A meeting(단수). in ~ Middle East까지는 꾸밈말이에요. 바로 앞 the Middle East나 cultures에 속지 마세요.' },
      { s: '{What|That} seemed like a priority last week when the agenda was crafted is not necessarily the priority right now — so discussion may branch off in a new direction.', ex: '정답 ▶ What\n이유: 선행사 없이 \'~했던 것\'이라는 명사절 주어 → What. 뒤 절에 주어가 빠져 있어요(___ seemed like a priority).' },
      { s: 'Or sub-groups may form within the room to discuss timely subjects {linked|linking} in some way to the main meeting trunk.', ex: '정답 ▶ linked\n이유: 주제가 줄기와 \'연결된\'(수동) → 과거분사 linked가 subjects를 꾸며요.' },
      { s: 'In flexible-time cultures, it seems clear that the most productive meetings grow in unpredictable ways, and the effective manager is flexible and professional {enough to|to enough} capitalize on priorities and changing needs as they arise.', ex: '정답 ▶ enough to\n이유: 형용사 + enough to + 동사원형 = ~할 만큼 충분히 …한. enough는 형용사 \'뒤\'에!' },
      { s: 'If we do it {while|during} we are eating, our multi-tasking is overloading us.', ex: '정답 ▶ while\n이유: 뒤에 \'주어 + 동사(we are eating)\'가 오므로 접속사 while\n오답 ▶ during은 전치사 → 뒤에 명사 (during the meal)' },
      { s: 'As our will attempts {to digest|digesting} the virtual content its forces are diverted from the physical content, our food.', ex: '정답 ▶ to digest\n이유: attempt + to부정사 = ~하려고 시도하다' },
      { s: 'It\'s easy {to eat|eating} a sandwich in a minute while talking on the phone.', ex: '정답 ▶ to eat\n이유: It(가주어) is easy to부정사(진주어) = ~하는 것은 쉽다' },
      { s: 'Some people are skilled at {doing|do} both at the same time.', ex: '정답 ▶ doing\n이유: 전치사 at 뒤에는 동명사. be skilled at -ing = ~하는 데 능숙하다' },
      { s: 'Eat your sandwich. Taste it. Let it {go|to go} down. Then text.', ex: '정답 ▶ go\n이유: 사역동사 let + 목적어 + 동사원형 = (목적어)가 ~하게 두다\n오답 ▶ to go ✘' }
    ] },
    { type: 'verb', title: '동사변형', guide: '( ) 안의 동사를 알맞은 형태로 바꿔 쓰세요.', items: [
      { s: 'An agenda with a meeting start time and a topic will probably be ___ before the meeting.', v: 'circulate', a: ['circulated'], ex: '정답 ▶ circulated\n이유: 의제는 \'배부되는\' 대상 → 수동 will be + p.p.' },
      { s: 'This will ___ as the trunk of the tree.', v: 'serve', a: ['serve'], ex: '정답 ▶ serve\n이유: 조동사 will 뒤 동사원형. serve as ~ = ~의 역할을 하다' },
      { s: 'Interruptions, agenda changes, and frequent shifts in direction are ___ as natural and necessary.', v: 'see', a: ['seen'], ex: '정답 ▶ seen\n이유: be seen as ~ = ~로 여겨지다 (수동). see - saw - seen' },
      { s: 'As our will attempts to digest the virtual content its forces are ___ from the physical content, our food.', v: 'divert', a: ['diverted'], ex: '정답 ▶ diverted\n이유: 힘(forces)이 \'돌려지는\' 것 → 수동 are + p.p.' },
      { s: 'It\'s easy to eat a sandwich in a minute while ___ on the phone.', v: 'talk', a: ['talking'], ex: '정답 ▶ talking\n이유: while (we are) talking → 접속사 + 현재분사 (주어가 \'말하는\' 능동)' },
      { s: 'The result can be a ___ text with the need for further clarifying texts and the beginnings of nervous indigestion.', v: 'misunderstand', a: ['misunderstood'], ex: '정답 ▶ misunderstood\n이유: 문자가 \'오해받는\'(수동) → 과거분사가 명사 text를 꾸며요. misunderstand - misunderstood - misunderstood' }
    ] },
    { type: 'error', title: '오류찾기', guide: '어법상 틀린 곳을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'But there\'s no expectation [[that]] the meeting will [[progress]] in a [[linearly]] manner.', w: 2, fix: 'linear', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ③ linearly → linear\n이유: 명사 manner를 꾸미는 자리 → 형용사 linear\n나머지 ▶ ① that(expectation의 내용을 설명하는 동격 that) ✔ ② will + 동사원형 ✔' },
      { s: 'Other branches may [[sprout]] as some members have urgent phone calls that [[takes]] them in and out of [[the meeting]].', w: 1, fix: 'take', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ② takes → take\n이유: that의 선행사는 phone calls(복수) → take\n나머지 ▶ ① may + 동사원형 ✔ ③ in and out of the meeting ✔' },
      { s: 'In flexible-time cultures, [[it]] seems clear that the most productive meetings [[grows]] in unpredictable ways, and the effective manager is flexible and professional enough to capitalize on priorities and changing needs as they [[arise]].', w: 1, fix: 'grow', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ② grows → grow\n이유: 주어 the most productive meetings(복수) → grow\n나머지 ▶ ① it(가주어, that절이 진주어) ✔ ③ arise(they = priorities and needs) ✔' },
      { s: 'Others [[write]] a half-hearted, shorthand text while they [[hurried]] eat their sandwich at the same [[time]].', w: 1, fix: 'hurriedly', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ② hurried → hurriedly\n이유: 동사 eat을 꾸미는 자리 → 부사 hurriedly(급하게)\n나머지 ▶ ① write(주어 Others 복수) ✔ ③ at the same time(동시에) ✔' },
      { s: 'Some people [[is]] skilled at [[doing]] [[both]] at the same time.', w: 0, fix: 'are', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ① is → are\n이유: 주어 Some people(복수) → are\n나머지 ▶ ② 전치사 at + 동명사 ✔ ③ both(둘 다) ✔' },
      { s: 'Eat your sandwich. [[Taste]] it. Let it go [[down]]. Then [[texting]].', w: 2, fix: 'text', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ③ texting → text\n이유: Eat, Taste, Let과 나란히 오는 명령문 → 동사원형 text(문자를 보내다)\n나머지 ▶ ① Taste(명령문) ✔ ② go down(넘어가다) ✔' }
    ] },
    { type: 'scramble', title: '본문 스크램블', guide: '우리말에 맞게 단어를 눌러 문장을 완성하세요.', items: [
      { ko: '이것이 나무의 줄기 역할을 할 것이다.', en: 'This will serve as the trunk of the tree.' },
      { ko: '하지만 회의가 일직선으로 진행될 것이라는 기대는 없다.', en: 'But there\'s no expectation that the meeting will progress in a linear manner.' },
      { ko: '중단, 의제 변경, 잦은 방향 전환은 자연스럽고 필요한 것으로 여겨진다.', en: 'Interruptions, agenda changes, and frequent shifts in direction are seen as natural and necessary.' },
      { ko: '만약 우리가 음식을 먹는 동안 그것을 한다면, 우리의 멀티태스킹은 우리에게 과부하를 주고 있는 것이다.', en: 'If we do it while we are eating, our multi-tasking is overloading us.' },
      { ko: '전화 통화를 하면서 1분 만에 샌드위치를 먹는 것은 쉽다.', en: 'It\'s easy to eat a sandwich in a minute while talking on the phone.' },
      { ko: '어떤 사람들은 두 가지를 동시에 하는 데 능숙하다.', en: 'Some people are skilled at doing both at the same time.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'order', title: '문장순서', guide: '문장을 본문 순서대로 눌러서 배열하세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['An agenda with a meeting start time and a topic will probably be circulated before the meeting.', 'This will serve as the trunk of the tree.', 'But there\'s no expectation that the meeting will progress in a linear manner.', 'What seemed like a priority last week when the agenda was crafted is not necessarily the priority right now — so discussion may branch off in a new direction.'], flow: ['회의 전에 의제 배부', '의제 = 나무의 줄기', '하지만 일직선 진행은 기대하지 않음', '우선순위가 바뀌어 논의가 새 가지로'] },
      { lines: ['Other branches may sprout as some members have urgent phone calls that take them in and out of the meeting.', 'Or sub-groups may form within the room to discuss timely subjects linked in some way to the main meeting trunk.', 'In flexible-time cultures, it seems clear that the most productive meetings grow in unpredictable ways, and the effective manager is flexible and professional enough to capitalize on priorities and changing needs as they arise.', 'Interruptions, agenda changes, and frequent shifts in direction are seen as natural and necessary.'], flow: ['긴급 전화로 다른 가지가 싹틈', '소그룹이 생겨 관련 주제 논의', '생산적 회의는 예측 불가하게 성장', '중단·변경은 자연스럽고 필요'] },
      { lines: ['When we are "always on" we are constantly ingesting content — reading alerts and texts, liking, checking emails, downloading, uploading, upgrading, updating, deleting, submitting responses, and posting.', 'If we do it while we are eating, our multi-tasking is overloading us.', 'As our will attempts to digest the virtual content its forces are diverted from the physical content, our food.'], flow: ['항상 접속 = 끊임없는 콘텐츠 섭취', '먹으면서 하면 과부하', '의지의 힘이 음식에서 멀어짐'] },
      { lines: ['Some people are skilled at doing both at the same time.', 'Others write a half-hearted, shorthand text while they hurriedly eat their sandwich at the same time.', 'The result can be a misunderstood text with the need for further clarifying texts and the beginnings of nervous indigestion.', 'Eat your sandwich. Taste it. Let it go down. Then text.'], flow: ['어떤 사람은 둘 다 능숙', '다른 사람은 급히 먹으며 성의 없는 문자', '결과: 오해 + 소화 불량', '먹고, 맛보고, 그다음에 문자하라'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'insert', title: '문장삽입', guide: '주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 알맞은 곳을 고르세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['An agenda with a meeting start time and a topic will probably be circulated before the meeting.', 'This will serve as the trunk of the tree.', 'But there\'s no expectation that the meeting will progress in a linear manner.'], given: 1, flow: ['회의 전에 의제가 배부됨', '이것(This) = 나무의 줄기 역할', '하지만 일직선 진행은 기대하지 않음'], ex: '정답 ▶ ①\n이유: This는 바로 앞의 agenda(의제)를 가리켜요. 줄기(의제)를 먼저 세우고 → But 가지는 예측 없이 뻗는다로 이어져요.' },
      { lines: ['It\'s easy to eat a sandwich in a minute while talking on the phone.', 'Some people are skilled at doing both at the same time.', 'Others write a half-hearted, shorthand text while they hurriedly eat their sandwich at the same time.'], given: 1, flow: ['통화하며 1분 만에 샌드위치 먹기는 쉬움', '어떤(Some) 사람은 둘 다 능숙함', '다른(Others) 사람은 성의 없는 문자를 씀'], ex: '정답 ▶ ①\n이유: Some people ~ / Others ~ 짝이에요. \'Some\'이 먼저 나와야 \'Others\'가 이어질 수 있어요.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'odd', title: '어색한 문장', guide: '글의 흐름과 관계없는 문장을 고르세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['What seemed like a priority last week when the agenda was crafted is not necessarily the priority right now — so discussion may branch off in a new direction.', 'Other branches may sprout as some members have urgent phone calls that take them in and out of the meeting.', 'Most meetings in these cultures start and end exactly on time.', 'Or sub-groups may form within the room to discuss timely subjects linked in some way to the main meeting trunk.'], odd: 2, ex: '정답 ▶ ③\n이유: 이 글은 유연한 시간 문화의 회의가 예측 없이 바뀐다는 내용이에요. \'정확히 제시간에 시작하고 끝난다\'는 흐름과 반대예요.' },
      { lines: ['If we do it while we are eating, our multi-tasking is overloading us.', 'As our will attempts to digest the virtual content its forces are diverted from the physical content, our food.', 'Online shopping has made buying food much more convenient.', 'It\'s easy to eat a sandwich in a minute while talking on the phone.'], odd: 2, ex: '정답 ▶ ③\n이유: 식사 중 멀티태스킹의 문제를 다루는 글이에요. \'온라인 쇼핑으로 음식 사기가 편해졌다\'는 관계없는 내용이에요.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'blank', title: '빈칸추론', guide: '빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'But there\'s no expectation that the meeting will progress in a ____ manner.', opts: ['linear', 'flexible', 'natural', 'friendly'], ex: '정답 ▶ linear\n이유: But(하지만)으로 앞의 \'정해진 의제\'와 대비돼요. 의제대로 \'일직선으로\' 진행되진 않는다는 뜻' },
      { s: 'In flexible-time cultures, it seems clear that the most productive meetings grow in ____ ways, and the effective manager is flexible and professional enough to capitalize on priorities and changing needs as they arise.', opts: ['unpredictable', 'fixed', 'planned', 'identical'], ex: '정답 ▶ unpredictable\n이유: 가지가 새 방향으로 뻗고 싹트듯, 생산적인 회의는 \'예측할 수 없는\' 방식으로 자라요.' },
      { s: 'If we do it while we are eating, our multi-tasking is ____ us.', opts: ['overloading', 'relaxing', 'protecting', 'feeding'], ex: '정답 ▶ overloading\n이유: 먹으면서 온갖 온라인 활동을 하면 \'과부하\'가 걸린다는 흐름이에요.' },
      { s: 'The result can be a misunderstood text with the need for further clarifying texts and the beginnings of nervous ____.', opts: ['indigestion', 'laughter', 'friendship', 'sleep'], ex: '정답 ▶ indigestion\n이유: 급하게 먹은 결과 → 신경성 \'소화 불량\'' }
    ] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '핵심문법', guide: '알맞은 답을 고르세요.', items: [
      { n: 21, q: '밑줄 친 <b>This</b>가 가리키는 것은?<br><b>An agenda will probably be circulated before the meeting. <u>This</u> will serve as the trunk of the tree.</b>', opts: ['the agenda (의제)', 'the meeting', 'the culture', 'the tree'], ex: '정답 ▶ the agenda\n이유: 회의 시작 시간과 주제가 담긴 의제가 나무의 \'줄기\'처럼 기본 틀이 돼요.' },
      { n: 21, q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>She is old ______ to drive a car.</b>', opts: ['enough', 'so', 'too', 'very'], ex: '정답 ▶ enough\n이유: 형용사 + enough to + 동사원형 = ~할 만큼 충분히 …하다\n비교 ▶ too ~ to … = 너무 ~해서 …할 수 없다' },
      { n: 21, q: '밑줄 친 부분의 뜻으로 알맞은 것은?<br><b>It is <u>not necessarily</u> the priority right now.</b>', opts: ['반드시 ~인 것은 아닌', '절대 ~이 아닌', '필요 없는', '당연히 ~인'], ex: '정답 ▶ 반드시 ~인 것은 아닌 (부분 부정)\n이유: not + necessarily/always/all = 부분 부정 \'반드시/항상/모두 ~인 것은 아니다\'' },
      { n: 22, q: '밑줄 친 <b>it</b>이 가리키는 것으로 알맞은 것은?<br><b>If we do <u>it</u> while we are eating, our multi-tasking is overloading us.</b>', opts: ['항상 접속해 콘텐츠를 섭취하는 것', '샌드위치를 먹는 것', '전화 통화를 하는 것', '음식을 소화하는 것'], ex: '정답 ▶ 항상 접속해 콘텐츠를 섭취하는 것\n이유: it은 앞 문장의 알림 읽기, 좋아요 누르기, 이메일 확인 등(constantly ingesting content)을 가리켜요.' },
      { n: 22, q: '밑줄 친 <b>its</b>가 가리키는 것은?<br><b>As our will attempts to digest the virtual content <u>its</u> forces are diverted from the physical content.</b>', opts: ['our will (우리의 의지)', 'the virtual content', 'our food', 'the phone'], ex: '정답 ▶ our will\n이유: 의지가 가상 콘텐츠를 소화하려 애쓰는 동안, 그 \'의지의\' 힘이 음식에서 멀어져요.' },
      { n: 22, q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>My mom let me ______ my friends after school.</b>', opts: ['meet', 'to meet', 'meeting', 'met'], ex: '정답 ▶ meet\n이유: let + 목적어 + 동사원형 (사역동사)\n본문 ▶ Let it go down.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '요약문 완성', guide: '지문을 읽고 요약문의 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말을 고르세요.', items: [
      { n: 21, box: ['21번 · 함축 의미'], q: '다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것은?<br><b>In flexible-time cultures, meetings start with an agenda but (A) ______ in unpredictable directions, which is considered (B) ______.</b>', opts: ['(A) branch out – (B) natural', '(A) branch out – (B) unacceptable', '(A) stay fixed – (B) natural', '(A) stay fixed – (B) unacceptable', '(A) end early – (B) rude'], ex: '정답 ▶ (A) branch out – (B) natural\n(A) 논의가 새 방향으로 \'가지를 뻗는다\' → branch out\n(B) 중단·변경은 \'자연스럽고 필요한\' 것으로 여겨짐 → natural\n오답 ▶ stay fixed(고정되다)·unacceptable(용납할 수 없는)은 반대예요.', ev: ['so discussion may branch off in a new direction', 'are seen as natural and necessary'] },
      { n: 22, box: ['22번 · 글의 요지'], q: '다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것은?<br><b>Doing online activities while eating (A) ______ us, so we should (B) ______ on our food first.</b>', opts: ['(A) overloads – (B) focus', '(A) overloads – (B) save', '(A) relaxes – (B) focus', '(A) relaxes – (B) spend', '(A) helps – (B) comment'], ex: '정답 ▶ (A) overloads – (B) focus\n(A) 먹으면서 온라인 활동 → 과부하 → overloads\n(B) 먼저 먹고 맛보고 그다음에 문자 → 음식에 \'집중\' → focus\n오답 ▶ relaxes·helps는 반대, save·spend·comment는 글과 관계없어요.', ev: ['our multi-tasking is overloading us', 'Eat your sandwich. Taste it'] }
    ] }
  ] },
  { id: 'd3', name: 'DAY 3', range: '23·24번', story: '23 글의 주제 · 24 제목', passage: [['#', '23번 · 글의 주제', ''], ['', 'Music can bring us into a very comfortable rhythm, and it can be satisfying in and of itself.', '음악은 우리를 매우 편안한 리듬으로 이끌 수 있고, 그 자체로 만족감을 줄 수 있다.'], ['', 'Most of the time, however, we want something more to happen.', '하지만 대부분의 경우 우리는 그 이상의 무언가가 일어나기를 원한다.'], ['', 'There needs to be a process that creates tension and drives the movement towards something in particular.', '긴장감을 만들고 특정한 무언가를 향해 악장을 몰고 가는 과정이 있어야 한다.'], ['', 'The excitement that arises in this process creates satisfaction and pleasure.', '이 과정에서 생기는 흥분이 만족감과 즐거움을 만들어 낸다.'], ['', 'This is seen in the measureless prelude as well.', '이것은 마디가 없는 전주곡에서도 보인다.'], ['', 'But there the tension might be caused by the ambiguity of the rhythm.', '하지만 그곳에서 긴장감은 리듬의 모호함에 의해 생길 수 있다.'], ['', 'Therefore, the fluid movement in the prelude usually creates a tension that can be released in the subsequent movement with a set rhythm.', '그래서 전주곡의 유동적인 움직임은 보통, 정해진 리듬이 있는 다음 악장에서 해소될 수 있는 긴장감을 만든다.'], ['', 'This, in turn, creates new and other types of tensions, where the rhythm is unambiguous and predictable, but where unexpected chords and modulations create drama and an experience of development in the piece.', '이것은 다시 새롭고 다른 종류의 긴장감을 만드는데, 그곳에서는 리듬이 분명하고 예측 가능하지만, 예상치 못한 화음과 전조가 극적인 효과와 곡이 전개되는 경험을 만들어 낸다.'], ['#', '24번 · 제목', ''], ['', 'Progressive educators and librarians are developing curricula to teach what we might call attention literacy.', '진보적인 교육자들과 사서들은 우리가 주의력 문해력이라 부를 만한 것을 가르치기 위한 교육과정을 개발하고 있다.'], ['', 'This means helping students recognize, for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site is deliberately designed to distract or provoke them.', '예를 들어, 이것은 소셜 미디어 피드나 뉴스 사이트의 구조가 학생들을 산만하게 하거나 자극하도록 어떻게 의도적으로 설계되었는지 그들이 인식하도록 돕는 것을 의미한다.'], ['', 'Classes in media literacy traditionally cover how to evaluate the credibility of sources — now they also include recognizing clickbait, understanding the role of algorithms in what content is served up, and observing one\'s own patterns of distraction.', '미디어 문해력 수업은 전통적으로 어떻게 정보원의 신뢰성을 평가하는지 다루었지만, 이제는 클릭베이트를 인식하고, 어떤 콘텐츠가 제공되는 것에 있어 알고리즘의 역할을 이해하며, 자신만의 주의 산만 패턴을 관찰하는 것도 포함한다.'], ['', 'A student might be tasked with tracking how many times they switch tasks or check their phone in an hour of homework, to raise self-awareness about fragmentation.', '학생은 (주의력) 파편화에 대한 자기 인식을 높이기 위해, 숙제하는 한 시간에 얼마나 여러 번 작업을 전환하거나 전화기를 확인하는지 추적하는 과제를 받을 수도 있다.'], ['', 'Another lesson might involve unpacking the design of popular apps: Why does an app use infinite scrolling instead of pages?', '또 다른 수업은 인기 앱들의 설계를 분석하는 것을 포함할 수 있다. 왜 앱은 페이지 대신 무한 스크롤을 사용할까?'], ['', 'Why do notifications appear with specific colors and sounds?', '왜 알림은 특정한 색상과 소리로 나타날까?'], ['', 'By treating these as deliberate design choices (often rooted in psychology and profit motives), young people become more critical users rather than passive consumers.', '이러한 것들을 (흔히 심리학과 이윤 동기에 뿌리를 둔) 의도적인 디자인 선택으로 취급함으로써, 젊은이들은 수동적인 소비자가 아니라 더 비판적인 사용자가 된다.']], steps: [
    { type: 'vocab', title: '핵심어휘·숙어', quizN: 15, items: [['rhythm', '리듬', 23], ['satisfying', '만족스러운', 23], ['in and of itself', '그 자체로', 23], ['tension', '긴장(감)', 23], ['drive', '몰아가다, 추진하다', 23], ['movement', '(음악의) 악장; 움직임', 23], ['in particular', '특정한, 특히', 23], ['arise', '생기다', 23], ['measureless', '마디(박자 구분)가 없는', 23], ['prelude', '전주곡', 23], ['ambiguity', '모호함', 23], ['fluid', '유동적인', 23], ['release', '해소하다, 풀어 주다', 23], ['subsequent', '뒤따르는, 다음의', 23], ['set', '정해진', 23], ['in turn', '이번에는, 결과적으로', 23], ['predictable', '예측 가능한', 23], ['chord', '화음', 23], ['modulation', '(음악의) 전조', 23], ['development', '전개, 발전', 23], ['progressive', '진보적인', 24], ['curriculum (pl. curricula)', '교육과정', 24], ['literacy', '문해력', 24], ['architecture', '구조, 설계', 24], ['deliberately', '의도적으로', 24], ['distract', '산만하게 하다', 24], ['provoke', '자극하다, 도발하다', 24], ['credibility', '신뢰성', 24], ['serve up', '제공하다', 24], ['distraction', '주의 산만', 24], ['be tasked with', '~의 임무를 맡다', 24], ['fragmentation', '파편화', 24], ['unpack', '분석하다, 풀어내다', 24], ['infinite', '무한한', 24], ['notification', '알림', 24], ['rooted in', '~에 뿌리를 둔', 24], ['profit motive', '이윤 동기', 24], ['passive', '수동적인', 24]] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '실전 문항', guide: '시험지 원문항이에요. 지문을 읽고 답을 고르세요.', items: [
      { q: '<b>23. 다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">Music can bring us into a very comfortable rhythm, and it can be satisfying in and of itself. Most of the time, however, we want something more to happen. There needs to be a process that creates tension and drives the movement towards something in particular. The excitement that arises in this process creates satisfaction and pleasure. This is seen in the measureless prelude as well. But there the tension might be caused by the ambiguity of the rhythm. Therefore, the fluid movement in the prelude usually creates a tension that can be released in the subsequent movement with a set rhythm. This, in turn, creates new and other types of tensions, where the rhythm is unambiguous and predictable, but where unexpected chords and modulations create drama and an experience of development in the piece.<br><small>* prelude: 전주곡 ** ambiguity: 모호함 *** modulation: (음악의) 전조</small></span>', opts: ['① roles of tension in musical progression', '② importance of rhythm and melody in playing music', '③ problems of overly considering audience expectations', '④ relationship between tension and musicians\' performance', '⑤ necessity of comfortable rhythms in musical development'], ex: '정답 ▶ ① roles of tension in musical progression (음악의 진행에서 긴장감의 역할)\n이유: 편안한 리듬만으로는 부족하고, 긴장감이 만들어지고 해소되며 다시 새 긴장감이 생기는 과정이 음악을 앞으로 \'진행\'시키고 만족감과 극적 효과를 준다는 글이에요.\n오답 ▶ ② 리듬과 멜로디의 중요성: 핵심은 \'긴장감\' / ③ 청중의 기대 이야기 없음 / ④ 연주자의 연주와의 관계 ✘ — 곡의 구조 이야기 (매력 오답) / ⑤ 편안한 리듬의 필요성: 오히려 \'그 이상\'이 필요하다고 함\n포인트 ▶ 글 전체에 tension이 6번 반복! 반복되는 핵심어가 주제예요.', per: [['음악 진행에서 긴장감의 역할', ['There needs to be a process that creates tension', 'creates new and other types of tensions'], '정답'], ['연주에서 리듬과 멜로디의 중요성', '', '✘ 핵심어는 tension'], ['청중의 기대를 지나치게 고려하는 문제', '', '✘ 언급 없음'], ['긴장감과 연주자의 연주 사이의 관계', '', '✘ 연주자 이야기 아님'], ['음악 발전에서 편안한 리듬의 필요성', 'we want something more to happen', '✘ 편안함 이상이 필요']], n: 23 },
      { n: 24, q: '<b>24. 다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">Progressive educators and librarians are developing curricula to teach what we might call attention literacy. This means helping students recognize, for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site is deliberately designed to distract or provoke them. Classes in media literacy traditionally cover how to evaluate the credibility of sources — now they also include recognizing clickbait, understanding the role of algorithms in what content is served up, and observing one\'s own patterns of distraction. A student might be tasked with tracking how many times they switch tasks or check their phone in an hour of homework, to raise self-awareness about fragmentation. Another lesson might involve unpacking the design of popular apps: Why does an app use infinite scrolling instead of pages? Why do notifications appear with specific colors and sounds? By treating these as deliberate design choices (often rooted in psychology and profit motives), young people become more critical users rather than passive consumers.</span>', opts: ['Raising Media Awareness: Noticing Intended Manipulations', 'Can Students Learn to Identify the Credibility of Information?', 'Application Developers\' Focus on Users\' Activeness', 'The Dark Side of Algorithms: A Threat to Privacy', 'Using Social Media to Communicate: Treasure or Poison?'], ex: '정답 ② · 매체가 학생을 산만하게 하도록 \'의도적으로 설계된(deliberately designed)\' 방식을 알아차리게 하는 주의력 문해력 교육 → 의도된 조작을 알아차리기', per: [['미디어 인식 높이기: 의도된 조작 알아차리기', ['is deliberately designed to distract or provoke them', 'By treating these as deliberate design choices'], '의도적 설계를 알아차려 비판적 사용자가 됨 → 정답'], ['학생들은 정보의 신뢰성을 판별하는 법을 배울 수 있는가?', 'traditionally cover how to evaluate the credibility of sources', '신뢰성 평가는 \'전통적인\' 수업 내용일 뿐, 핵심이 아님 (오답률 1위 매력 선지)'], ['앱 개발자들의 사용자 능동성에 대한 관심', 'often rooted in psychology and profit motives', '개발자는 오히려 이윤을 위해 산만하게 만듦'], ['알고리즘의 어두운 면: 사생활 위협', '', '사생활 이야기는 없음'], ['소통을 위한 소셜 미디어: 보물인가 독인가?', '', '소통 수단으로서의 SNS 평가가 아님']] }
    ] },
    { type: 'pick', title: '어휘선택', guide: '문맥에 맞는 낱말을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'There needs to be a process that creates {tension|attention} and drives the movement towards something in particular.', ex: '정답 ▶ tension (긴장감)\n오답 ▶ attention (주의): 이 글의 핵심어. 긴장감을 만들어 악장을 앞으로 몰고 가요.' },
      { s: 'The excitement that arises in this process creates {satisfaction|dissatisfaction} and pleasure.', ex: '정답 ▶ satisfaction (만족감)\n오답 ▶ dissatisfaction (불만족): 흥분이 만족감과 \'즐거움(pleasure)\'을 만든다 → 같은 방향의 말' },
      { s: 'But there the tension might be caused by the {ambiguity|ambition} of the rhythm.', ex: '정답 ▶ ambiguity (모호함)\n오답 ▶ ambition (야망): 마디 없는 전주곡은 리듬이 \'모호해서\' 긴장감이 생겨요. 철자 혼동 주의!' },
      { s: 'Therefore, the fluid movement in the prelude usually creates a tension that can be released in the {subsequent|previous} movement with a set rhythm.', ex: '정답 ▶ subsequent (뒤따르는)\n오답 ▶ previous (이전의): 전주곡에서 만든 긴장이 \'다음\' 악장에서 풀려요.' },
      { s: 'This, in turn, creates new and other types of tensions, where the rhythm is unambiguous and {predictable|unpredictable}, but where unexpected chords and modulations create drama and an experience of development in the piece.', ex: '정답 ▶ predictable (예측 가능한)\n오답 ▶ unpredictable: unambiguous(분명한)와 and로 나란히 → 같은 방향. 바로 뒤 but 이후에 \'예상치 못한\' 화음이 대비돼요.' },
      { s: 'Progressive educators and librarians are developing curricula to teach what we might call attention {literacy|literature}.', ex: 'literacy 문해력 vs literature 문학' },
      { s: 'This means helping students recognize, for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site is {deliberately|accidentally} designed to distract or provoke them.', ex: '설계는 \'의도적으로\' → deliberately' },
      { s: 'Classes in media literacy traditionally cover how to evaluate the {credibility|incredibility} of sources — now they also include recognizing clickbait, understanding the role of algorithms in what content is served up, and observing one\'s own patterns of distraction.', ex: '정보원의 \'신뢰성\' 평가 → credibility' },
      { s: 'A student might be tasked with tracking how many times they switch tasks or check their phone in an hour of homework, to raise self-awareness about {fragmentation|concentration}.', ex: '작업 전환·폰 확인 = 주의가 쪼개짐 → fragmentation' },
      { s: 'By treating these as {deliberate|accidental} design choices (often rooted in psychology and profit motives), young people become more critical users rather than passive consumers.', ex: '이윤 동기에 뿌리 → 의도적인(deliberate) 선택' },
      { s: 'By treating these as deliberate design choices (often rooted in psychology and profit motives), young people become more critical users rather than {passive|active} consumers.', ex: 'critical users와 대비 → passive' }
    ] },
    { type: 'tf', title: 'T/F 내용 확인', guide: '본문 내용과 일치하면 T, 다르면 F를 고르세요.', items: [['Music with a comfortable rhythm can be satisfying on its own.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: it can be satisfying in and of itself\n어휘 ▶ on its own = in and of itself 그 자체로', 23, 'it can be satisfying in and of itself'], ['We usually want nothing more than a comfortable rhythm in music.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 대부분의 경우 \'그 이상의 무언가\'가 일어나기를 원해요.', 23, 'we want something more to happen'], ['In the measureless prelude, tension can come from an unclear rhythm.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: 긴장이 리듬의 모호함(ambiguity)에서 생길 수 있어요.\n어휘 ▶ unclear = ambiguous', 23, 'caused by the ambiguity of the rhythm'], ['Once the rhythm becomes predictable, all tension disappears from the piece.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 리듬이 예측 가능해져도 화음과 전조가 \'새로운 종류의 긴장감\'을 만들어요.', 23, 'creates new and other types of tensions'], ['Educators are designing courses to cultivate what could be termed attention literacy.', true, 'design courses = develop curricula · be termed = be called', 24, 'developing curricula to teach what we might call attention literacy'], ['Media literacy classes now focus solely on judging the reliability of sources.', false, 'solely 오로지 · reliability = credibility → 이제는 클릭베이트·알고리즘·주의 산만 관찰도 포함', 24, 'now they also include recognizing clickbait'], ['Students may be asked to monitor how often they switch tasks during homework.', true, 'monitor = track · how often = how many times', 24, 'tracking how many times they switch tasks'], ['The writer believes app features like infinite scrolling are accidental.', false, 'accidental 우연한 → 의도적인 디자인 선택(deliberate design choices)', 24, 'By treating these as deliberate design choices']] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '일치·불일치', guide: '선택지는 본문을 어려운 어휘로 바꿔 쓴 문장이에요. 풀고 나서 해설의 어휘를 꼭 확인하세요.', items: [
      { q: '글의 내용과 <u>일치하는</u> 것은?', opts: ['Tension built in the prelude may be released in the next movement.', 'Excitement in music reduces our pleasure.', 'The measureless prelude always has a clear rhythm.', 'Unexpected chords make a piece less dramatic.', 'Tension is unnecessary for musical development.'], ex: '정답 ▶ Tension built in the prelude may be released in the next movement.\n이유: 전주곡에서 만든 긴장감은 다음 악장에서 해소될 수 있어요.\n어휘 ▶ build 쌓다 · release 해소하다', per: [['전주곡에서 쌓인 긴장감은 다음 악장에서 해소될 수 있다.', 'can be released in the subsequent movement', '일치'], ['음악의 흥분은 즐거움을 줄인다.', 'creates satisfaction and pleasure', '불일치 → 즐거움을 만듦'], ['마디 없는 전주곡은 늘 분명한 리듬을 갖는다.', 'the ambiguity of the rhythm', '불일치 → 모호함'], ['예상치 못한 화음은 곡을 덜 극적으로 만든다.', 'unexpected chords and modulations create drama', '불일치 → 극적 효과를 만듦'], ['긴장감은 음악 전개에 불필요하다.', 'There needs to be a process that creates tension', '불일치 → 필요함']], n: 23, box: ['23번 · 글의 주제'] },
      { n: 24, q: '<b>24. 글의 내용과 일치하지 <u>않는</u> 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">Progressive educators and librarians are developing curricula to teach what we might call attention literacy. This means helping students recognize, for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site is deliberately designed to distract or provoke them. Classes in media literacy traditionally cover how to evaluate the credibility of sources — now they also include recognizing clickbait, understanding the role of algorithms in what content is served up, and observing one\'s own patterns of distraction. A student might be tasked with tracking how many times they switch tasks or check their phone in an hour of homework, to raise self-awareness about fragmentation. Another lesson might involve unpacking the design of popular apps: Why does an app use infinite scrolling instead of pages? Why do notifications appear with specific colors and sounds? By treating these as deliberate design choices (often rooted in psychology and profit motives), young people become more critical users rather than passive consumers.</span>', opts: ['Attention literacy classes encourage students to remain passive consumers of media.', 'The design of news sites and social media feeds can be intended to distract users.', 'Media literacy classes have traditionally dealt with assessing the credibility of sources.', 'Students might keep a record of how frequently they check their phones while studying.', 'Many app design features stem from psychology and profit motives.'], ex: '정답: remain passive consumers 수동적 소비자로 남다 → 오히려 비판적 사용자가 됨\n· be intended to ~하도록 의도되다 = be deliberately designed to\n· deal with = cover · assess = evaluate\n· keep a record of = track · frequently = many times\n· stem from = be rooted in', per: [['주의력 문해력 수업은 학생들이 수동적 소비자로 남도록 장려한다.', 'young people become more critical users rather than passive consumers', '불일치'], ['뉴스 사이트와 SNS 피드의 설계는 사용자를 산만하게 하도록 의도될 수 있다.', 'is deliberately designed to distract or provoke them', '일치'], ['미디어 문해력 수업은 전통적으로 정보원의 신뢰성 평가를 다뤄 왔다.', 'traditionally cover how to evaluate the credibility of sources', '일치'], ['학생들은 공부하면서 휴대폰을 얼마나 자주 확인하는지 기록할 수도 있다.', 'tracking how many times they switch tasks or check their phone', '일치'], ['많은 앱 디자인 요소는 심리학과 이윤 동기에서 비롯된다.', 'often rooted in psychology and profit motives', '일치']] }
    ] },
    { type: 'pick', title: '어법선택', guide: '어법상 알맞은 것을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'Music can bring us into a very comfortable rhythm, and it can be {satisfying|satisfied} in and of itself.', ex: '정답 ▶ satisfying\n이유: 주어 it(음악)이 만족감을 \'주는\' 것 → -ing (satisfying 만족스러운)\n오답 ▶ satisfied는 사람이 만족감을 \'느낄\' 때' },
      { s: 'Most of the time, however, we want something more {to happen|happen}.', ex: '정답 ▶ to happen\n이유: want + 목적어 + to부정사 = (목적어)가 ~하기를 원하다' },
      { s: 'There {needs|need} to be a process that creates tension and drives the movement towards something in particular.', ex: '정답 ▶ needs\n이유: There + 동사 + 주어 → 진짜 주어는 a process(단수) → needs' },
      { s: 'The excitement that arises in this process {creates|create} satisfaction and pleasure.', ex: '정답 ▶ creates\n이유: 주어는 The excitement(단수). that ~ process는 주어를 꾸미는 관계절이에요. 바로 앞 process에 속지 마세요.' },
      { s: 'This, in turn, creates new and other types of tensions, {where|which} the rhythm is unambiguous and predictable, but where unexpected chords and modulations create drama and an experience of development in the piece.', ex: '정답 ▶ where\n이유: 뒤에 완전한 절(the rhythm is unambiguous)이 오므로 관계부사 where (그 긴장감 \'속에서\')\n오답 ▶ which 뒤에는 불완전한 절' },
      { s: 'Progressive educators and librarians are developing curricula to teach {what|that} we might call attention literacy.', ex: 'teach의 목적어 + call의 목적어가 빠진 절 → 관계대명사 what' },
      { s: 'This means {helping|to help} students recognize, for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site is deliberately designed to distract or provoke them.', ex: 'mean -ing: ~하는 것을 의미하다 (mean to: ~할 작정이다)' },
      { s: 'This means helping students recognize, for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site {is|are} deliberately designed to distract or provoke them.', ex: '주어 the architecture(단수) → is' },
      { s: 'Classes in media literacy traditionally cover how to evaluate the credibility of sources — now they also include recognizing clickbait, understanding the role of algorithms in what content {is served|serves} up, and observing one\'s own patterns of distraction.', ex: '콘텐츠가 \'제공되는\' 수동' },
      { s: 'A student might be tasked with {tracking|track} how many times they switch tasks or check their phone in an hour of homework, to raise self-awareness about fragmentation.', ex: '전치사 with + 동명사' },
      { s: 'By treating these as deliberate design choices (often {rooted|rooting} in psychology and profit motives), young people become more critical users rather than passive consumers.', ex: '\'뿌리를 둔\' 수동 → rooted' }
    ] },
    { type: 'verb', title: '동사변형', guide: '( ) 안의 동사를 알맞은 형태로 바꿔 쓰세요.', items: [
      { s: 'This is ___ in the measureless prelude as well.', v: 'see', a: ['seen'], ex: '정답 ▶ seen\n이유: 이것이 \'보이는\' 것 → 수동 is + p.p.' },
      { s: 'But there the tension might be ___ by the ambiguity of the rhythm.', v: 'cause', a: ['caused'], ex: '정답 ▶ caused\n이유: 긴장감이 모호함에 \'의해 생기는\' 것 → 수동 be + p.p. (뒤의 by가 힌트)' },
      { s: 'There needs to be a process that creates tension and ___ the movement towards something in particular.', v: 'drive', a: ['drives'], ex: '정답 ▶ drives\n이유: that creates ~ and drives ~ → 관계절의 동사 두 개가 병렬. 선행사 a process(단수) → drives' },
      { s: 'This means helping students recognize, for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site is deliberately ___ to distract or provoke them.', v: 'design', a: ['designed'], ex: 'be designed to ~하도록 설계되다' },
      { s: 'A student might be ___ with tracking how many times they switch tasks or check their phone in an hour of homework, to raise self-awareness about fragmentation.', v: 'task', a: ['tasked'], ex: 'be tasked with ~의 임무를 맡다' },
      { s: 'By ___ these as deliberate design choices (often rooted in psychology and profit motives), young people become more critical users rather than passive consumers.', v: 'treat', a: ['treating'], ex: 'By + 동명사' }
    ] },
    { type: 'error', title: '오류찾기', guide: '어법상 틀린 곳을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'Music can [[bring]] us into a very [[comfortable]] rhythm, and it can be satisfying in and of [[it]].', w: 2, fix: 'itself', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ③ it → itself\n이유: in and of itself = 그 자체로 (고정 표현, 재귀대명사)\n나머지 ▶ ① can + 동사원형 ✔ ② comfortable(명사 rhythm 수식) ✔' },
      { s: 'Therefore, the fluid movement in the prelude usually [[creates]] a tension [[what]] can be released in the [[subsequent]] movement with a set rhythm.', w: 1, fix: 'that', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ② what → that (또는 which)\n이유: 앞에 선행사 a tension이 있으니 관계대명사 that/which. what은 선행사가 없을 때만\n나머지 ▶ ① creates(주어 movement 단수) ✔ ③ subsequent ✔' },
      { s: 'But there the tension [[might]] [[be caused]] by the [[ambiguous]] of the rhythm.', w: 2, fix: 'ambiguity', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ③ ambiguous → ambiguity\n이유: the ~ of 사이는 명사 자리 → ambiguity(모호함)\n나머지 ▶ ① might(~할 수도 있다) ✔ ② be caused(수동) ✔' },
      { s: 'Progressive educators and librarians [[are developing]] curricula [[to teach]] [[that]] we might [[call]] attention literacy.', w: 2, fix: 'what', ex: 'teach의 목적어 역할 + 불완전한 절 → what' },
      { s: 'This means [[to help]] students [[recognize]], for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site [[is]] deliberately [[designed]] to distract or provoke them.', w: 0, fix: 'helping', ex: 'mean -ing (~하는 것을 의미하다)' },
      { s: 'By treating these as deliberate design choices (often [[rooting]] in psychology and profit motives), young people [[become]] more [[critical]] users rather than [[passive]] consumers.', w: 0, fix: 'rooted', ex: '\'뿌리를 둔\' 수동 → rooted' }
    ] },
    { type: 'scramble', title: '본문 스크램블', guide: '우리말에 맞게 단어를 눌러 문장을 완성하세요.', items: [
      { ko: '하지만 대부분의 경우 우리는 그 이상의 무언가가 일어나기를 원한다.', en: 'Most of the time, however, we want something more to happen.' },
      { ko: '이 과정에서 생기는 흥분이 만족감과 즐거움을 만들어 낸다.', en: 'The excitement that arises in this process creates satisfaction and pleasure.' },
      { ko: '하지만 그곳에서 긴장감은 리듬의 모호함에 의해 생길 수 있다.', en: 'But there the tension might be caused by the ambiguity of the rhythm.' },
      { ko: '진보적인 교육자들과 사서들은 우리가 주의력 문해력이라 부를 만한 것을 가르치기 위한 교육과정을 개발하고 있다.', en: 'Progressive educators and librarians are developing curricula to teach what we might call attention literacy.' },
      { ko: '왜 알림은 특정한 색상과 소리로 나타날까?', en: 'Why do notifications appear with specific colors and sounds?' }
    ] },
    { type: 'order', title: '문장순서', guide: '문장을 본문 순서대로 눌러서 배열하세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['Music can bring us into a very comfortable rhythm, and it can be satisfying in and of itself.', 'Most of the time, however, we want something more to happen.', 'There needs to be a process that creates tension and drives the movement towards something in particular.', 'The excitement that arises in this process creates satisfaction and pleasure.'], flow: ['편안한 리듬은 그 자체로 만족감', '하지만 그 이상을 원함', '긴장감을 만드는 과정이 필요', '그 흥분이 만족감과 즐거움을 줌'] },
      { lines: ['This is seen in the measureless prelude as well.', 'But there the tension might be caused by the ambiguity of the rhythm.', 'Therefore, the fluid movement in the prelude usually creates a tension that can be released in the subsequent movement with a set rhythm.', 'This, in turn, creates new and other types of tensions, where the rhythm is unambiguous and predictable, but where unexpected chords and modulations create drama and an experience of development in the piece.'], flow: ['마디 없는 전주곡에서도 보임', '거기선 리듬의 모호함이 긴장감을 만듦', '그 긴장이 다음 악장에서 해소', '다시 화음과 전조가 새 긴장감을 만듦'] },
      { lines: ['Progressive educators and librarians are developing curricula to teach what we might call attention literacy.', 'This means helping students recognize, for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site is deliberately designed to distract or provoke them.', 'Classes in media literacy traditionally cover how to evaluate the credibility of sources — now they also include recognizing clickbait, understanding the role of algorithms in what content is served up, and observing one\'s own patterns of distraction.', 'A student might be tasked with tracking how many times they switch tasks or check their phone in an hour of homework, to raise self-awareness about fragmentation.'], flow: ['교육자들이 주의력 문해력 교육과정을 개발 중', '의도적 설계를 인식하도록 돕는 것', '전통적 신뢰성 평가 + 클릭베이트·알고리즘·주의 산만 관찰', '예: 숙제 중 작업 전환·폰 확인 횟수 추적'] },
      { lines: ['A student might be tasked with tracking how many times they switch tasks or check their phone in an hour of homework, to raise self-awareness about fragmentation.', 'Another lesson might involve unpacking the design of popular apps: Why does an app use infinite scrolling instead of pages?', 'Why do notifications appear with specific colors and sounds?', 'By treating these as deliberate design choices (often rooted in psychology and profit motives), young people become more critical users rather than passive consumers.'], flow: ['작업 전환 횟수 추적', '또 다른 수업: 무한 스크롤은 왜?', '알림 색과 소리는 왜?', '의도적 설계로 보면 비판적 사용자가 됨'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'insert', title: '문장삽입', guide: '주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 알맞은 곳을 고르세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['The excitement that arises in this process creates satisfaction and pleasure.', 'This is seen in the measureless prelude as well.', 'But there the tension might be caused by the ambiguity of the rhythm.'], given: 1, flow: ['흥분이 만족감과 즐거움을 줌', '이것은 마디 없는 전주곡에서도 보임', '하지만 그곳(there)에서 긴장감은 리듬의 모호함에서 옴'], ex: '정답 ▶ ①\n이유: 마지막 문장의 there는 \'전주곡\'을 가리켜요. 그러니 전주곡을 처음 말하는 문장이 바로 앞에 와야 해요.' },
      { lines: ['This means helping students recognize, for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site is deliberately designed to distract or provoke them.', 'Classes in media literacy traditionally cover how to evaluate the credibility of sources — now they also include recognizing clickbait, understanding the role of algorithms in what content is served up, and observing one\'s own patterns of distraction.', 'A student might be tasked with tracking how many times they switch tasks or check their phone in an hour of homework, to raise self-awareness about fragmentation.', 'Another lesson might involve unpacking the design of popular apps: Why does an app use infinite scrolling instead of pages?'], given: 2, flow: ['의도적 설계 인식', '수업 내용 확장', '예: 작업 전환 추적 (A student ~)', '또 다른 수업(Another lesson)'], ex: 'Another lesson의 앞에는 첫 번째 수업 예시(A student ~)가 와야 한다.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'odd', title: '어색한 문장', guide: '글의 흐름과 관계없는 문장을 고르세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['Most of the time, however, we want something more to happen.', 'There needs to be a process that creates tension and drives the movement towards something in particular.', 'Listening to loud music for a long time can damage your hearing.', 'The excitement that arises in this process creates satisfaction and pleasure.'], odd: 2, ex: '정답 ▶ ③\n이유: 음악 속 긴장감의 역할을 다루는 글이에요. \'큰 음악이 청력을 해친다\'는 관계없는 내용이에요.' },
      { lines: ['Classes in media literacy traditionally cover how to evaluate the credibility of sources — now they also include recognizing clickbait, understanding the role of algorithms in what content is served up, and observing one\'s own patterns of distraction.', 'A student might be tasked with tracking how many times they switch tasks or check their phone in an hour of homework, to raise self-awareness about fragmentation.', 'Many schools now provide students with free laptops for online classes.', 'Another lesson might involve unpacking the design of popular apps: Why does an app use infinite scrolling instead of pages?'], odd: 2, ex: '노트북 지급 → 주의력 문해력 수업 내용과 관계없음' }
    ] },
    { type: 'blank', title: '빈칸추론', guide: '빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'There needs to be a process that creates ____ and drives the movement towards something in particular.', opts: ['tension', 'silence', 'comfort', 'boredom'], ex: '정답 ▶ tension\n이유: \'편안함 그 이상\'이 필요하다는 앞 문장과 이어져요. 악장을 앞으로 몰아가는 것은 \'긴장감\'' },
      { s: 'This, in turn, creates new and other types of tensions, where the rhythm is unambiguous and predictable, but where unexpected chords and modulations create ____ and an experience of development in the piece.', opts: ['drama', 'silence', 'rest', 'boredom'], ex: '정답 ▶ drama\n이유: 예상치 못한 화음과 전조 → 새 긴장감 → \'극적인 효과\'와 곡이 전개되는 경험' },
      { s: 'This means helping students recognize, for example, how the architecture of a social media feed or a news site is ____ to distract or provoke them.', opts: ['deliberately designed', 'accidentally created', 'carefully protected', 'rarely updated'], ex: '의도적 설계를 알아차리는 것이 핵심' },
      { s: 'By treating these as deliberate design choices (often rooted in psychology and profit motives), young people become ____.', opts: ['more critical users rather than passive consumers', 'more loyal customers of popular apps', 'less interested in technology', 'skilled app developers'], ex: '결론: 비판적 사용자' }
    ] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '핵심문법', guide: '알맞은 답을 고르세요.', items: [
      { n: 23, q: '밑줄 친 <b>there</b>가 가리키는 것은?<br><b>This is seen in the measureless prelude as well. But <u>there</u> the tension might be caused by the ambiguity of the rhythm.</b>', opts: ['the measureless prelude', 'this process', 'the subsequent movement', 'a comfortable rhythm'], ex: '정답 ▶ the measureless prelude\n이유: 바로 앞 문장의 \'마디 없는 전주곡\'에서는 긴장이 리듬의 모호함에서 생겨요.' },
      { n: 23, q: '밑줄 친 부분의 뜻으로 알맞은 것은?<br><b>It can be satisfying <u>in and of itself</u>.</b>', opts: ['그 자체로', '그 안에서만', '스스로 만든', '결국에는'], ex: '정답 ▶ 그 자체로\n이유: in and of itself = in itself = 그 자체로 (다른 것 없이도)' },
      { n: 23, q: '밑줄 친 <b>This</b>가 가리키는 것으로 알맞은 것은?<br><b>The fluid movement creates a tension that can be released in the subsequent movement. <u>This</u>, in turn, creates new types of tensions.</b>', opts: ['정해진 리듬의 다음 악장(에서의 해소)', '마디 없는 전주곡의 모호함', '편안한 리듬', '청중의 기대'], ex: '정답 ▶ 정해진 리듬의 다음 악장(에서의 해소)\n이유: 긴장이 해소된 다음 악장이 \'이번에는(in turn)\' 화음과 전조로 새로운 긴장을 만들어요. 긴장 → 해소 → 새 긴장의 순환' },
      { q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>They teach ______ we might call attention literacy.</b>', opts: ['what', 'that', 'which', 'how'], ex: '선행사 없는 불완전한 절 → what' },
      { q: '다음 문장의 뜻으로 알맞은 것은?<br><b>This means helping students recognize it.</b>', opts: ['이것은 학생들이 그것을 인식하도록 돕는 것을 의미한다.', '이것은 학생들이 그것을 인식하도록 도울 작정이다.', '이것은 학생들을 도와서 인식할 필요가 없다.', '이것은 학생들이 돕는 것을 인식한다는 뜻이다.'], ex: 'mean -ing: ~을 의미하다 / mean to: ~할 작정이다' },
      { q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>It is ______ in psychology and profit motives.</b>', opts: ['rooted', 'rooting', 'root', 'to root'], ex: 'be rooted in' }
    ] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '요약문 완성', guide: '지문을 읽고 요약문의 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말을 고르세요.', items: [
      { n: 23, box: ['23번 · 글의 주제'], q: '다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것은?<br><b>In music, (A) ______ is created and then released, which brings pleasure and a sense of (B) ______.</b>', opts: ['(A) tension – (B) development', '(A) tension – (B) boredom', '(A) silence – (B) development', '(A) silence – (B) boredom', '(A) comfort – (B) confusion'], ex: '정답 ▶ (A) tension – (B) development\n(A) 만들어지고 해소되는 것 = 긴장감 → tension\n(B) 즐거움과 함께 곡이 \'전개되는\' 경험 → development\n오답 ▶ boredom(지루함)·confusion(혼란)은 즐거움과 맞지 않아요.', ev: ['There needs to be a process that creates tension', 'an experience of development in the piece'] }
    ] }
  ] },
  { id: 'd4', name: 'DAY 4', range: '26·27번', story: '26 내용 불일치 · 27 안내문 불일치', passage: [['#', '26번 · 내용 불일치', ''], ['', 'Frederick Law Olmsted, known as the father of American landscape architecture, was born in 1822.', '미국 조경 건축의 아버지로 알려진 Frederick Law Olmsted는 1822년에 태어났다.'], ['', 'When Olmsted was young, he often went on trips to the countryside with his father, who loved scenery.', 'Olmsted는 어렸을 때 경치를 사랑했던 아버지와 함께 자주 시골로 여행을 다녔다.'], ['', 'At age 14, he was preparing himself to study at Yale College, but he could not enter due to a severe sumac poisoning that damaged his eyesight.', '14세에 그는 Yale College에서 공부할 준비를 하고 있었지만, 시력을 손상시킨 심한 옻나무 중독 때문에 입학할 수 없었다.'], ['', 'Instead of attending a college, he gained experience working as a clerk, a sailor, and a farmer.', '대학에 다니는 대신, 그는 점원, 선원, 농부로 일하며 경험을 쌓았다.'], ['', 'In 1858, he worked with Calvert Vaux and won the design competition for New York City\'s Central Park with the "Greensward Plan."', '1858년, 그는 Calvert Vaux와 함께 작업하여 \'그린스워드 계획\'으로 뉴욕시 Central Park 설계 공모전에서 당선되었다.'], ['', 'During the Civil War, Olmsted served as General Secretary of the United States Sanitary Commission.', '남북 전쟁 동안 Olmsted는 미국 위생 위원회의 사무총장으로 일했다.'], ['', 'Although he passed away in 1903, his sons and successors continued his design philosophy.', '그는 1903년에 세상을 떠났지만, 그의 아들들과 후계자들이 그의 디자인 철학을 이어 갔다.'], ['', 'In 1979, the National Park Service designated his home and office in Brookline, Massachusetts, as a National Historic Site to honor his legacy.', '1979년, 국립공원관리청은 그의 유산을 기리기 위해 매사추세츠주 Brookline에 있는 그의 집과 사무실을 국가 사적지로 지정했다.'], ['#', '27번 · 안내문 불일치', ''], ['', 'Ashwood Dunes Reforestation Event', 'Ashwood Dunes 숲 가꾸기 행사'], ['', 'Join us for a meaningful morning at the annual Ashwood Dunes Reforestation.', '매년 열리는 Ashwood Dunes 숲 가꾸기 행사에서 뜻깊은 아침을 함께해 주세요.'], ['', 'Volunteers will plant native tree seedlings to help restore the local environment.', '자원봉사자들은 지역 환경 복원을 돕기 위해 토종 나무 묘목을 심게 됩니다.'], ['', 'No special skills required, feel free to join us.', '특별한 기술은 필요 없으니, 부담 없이 함께해 주세요.'], ['', 'When and Where: September 5th, 2026, 8 a.m.-11 a.m., Ashwood Dunes Wilderness', '일시와 장소: 2026년 9월 5일 오전 8시~11시, Ashwood Dunes 황야 지대'], ['', 'Participation Fee: Free', '참가비: 무료'], ['', 'Rewards: All participants will get a commemorative badge.', '보상: 모든 참가자는 기념 배지를 받습니다.'], ['', 'Plant 5 trees: Receive a reusable water bottle', '나무 5그루를 심으면: 재사용 물병을 받으세요'], ['', 'Plant 10 or more trees: Receive a T-shirt', '10그루 이상 심으면: 티셔츠를 받으세요'], ['', 'Registration: Online registration is encouraged, but on-site sign-ups will be available on the day of the event.', '등록: 온라인 등록을 권장하지만, 행사 당일 현장 등록도 가능합니다.'], ['', 'Event Guidelines: Bring a hat and gloves; shovels and water will be prepared.', '행사 지침: 모자와 장갑을 가져오세요. 삽과 물은 준비되어 있습니다.'], ['', 'Follow instructions from the event staff.', '행사 진행 요원의 안내를 따라 주세요.'], ['', 'For more information, please visit www.ashwooddunes.com.', '더 많은 정보를 원하시면 www.ashwooddunes.com을 방문하세요.']], steps: [
    { type: 'vocab', title: '핵심어휘·숙어', quizN: 15, items: [['landscape architecture', '조경 건축', 26], ['countryside', '시골', 26], ['scenery', '경치, 풍경', 26], ['severe', '심각한', 26], ['poisoning', '중독', 26], ['eyesight', '시력', 26], ['attend', '(학교에) 다니다', 26], ['gain experience', '경험을 쌓다', 26], ['clerk', '점원, 사무원', 26], ['sailor', '선원', 26], ['competition', '공모전, 대회', 26], ['serve as', '~로 일하다, 근무하다', 26], ['pass away', '세상을 떠나다', 26], ['successor', '후계자', 26], ['philosophy', '철학', 26], ['designate A as B', 'A를 B로 지정하다', 26], ['honor', '기리다, 경의를 표하다', 26], ['legacy', '유산', 26], ['reforestation', '숲 가꾸기, 재조림', 27], ['meaningful', '뜻깊은', 27], ['annual', '매년의, 연례의', 27], ['volunteer', '자원봉사자', 27], ['native', '토종의', 27], ['seedling', '묘목', 27], ['restore', '복원하다', 27], ['required', '필요한, 요구되는', 27], ['feel free to', '부담 없이 ~하다', 27], ['wilderness', '황야, 자연 그대로의 지역', 27], ['participation fee', '참가비', 27], ['commemorative', '기념하는', 27], ['reusable', '재사용 가능한', 27], ['registration', '등록', 27], ['encourage', '권장하다', 27], ['on-site', '현장의', 27], ['sign-up', '등록, 신청', 27], ['shovel', '삽', 27]] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '실전 문항', guide: '시험지 원문항이에요. 지문을 읽고 답을 고르세요.', items: [
      { q: '<b>26. Frederick Law Olmsted에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하지 <u>않는</u> 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">Frederick Law Olmsted, known as the father of American landscape architecture, was born in 1822. When Olmsted was young, he often went on trips to the countryside with his father, who loved scenery. At age 14, he was preparing himself to study at Yale College, but he could not enter due to a severe sumac poisoning that damaged his eyesight. Instead of attending a college, he gained experience working as a clerk, a sailor, and a farmer. In 1858, he worked with Calvert Vaux and won the design competition for New York City\'s Central Park with the "Greensward Plan." During the Civil War, Olmsted served as General Secretary of the United States Sanitary Commission. Although he passed away in 1903, his sons and successors continued his design philosophy. In 1979, the National Park Service designated his home and office in Brookline, Massachusetts, as a National Historic Site to honor his legacy.<br><small>* sumac: 옻나무</small></span>', opts: ['② 14세에 Yale College에 입학했다.', '① 어렸을 때 아버지와 시골로 여행을 다녔다.', '③ 1858년에 뉴욕시 Central Park 설계 공모전에서 당선되었다.', '④ Civil War 기간 중 미국 Sanitary Commission에서 일했다.', '⑤ Brookline에 있는 집과 사무실이 National Historic Site로 지정되었다.'], ex: '정답 ▶ ② 14세에 Yale College에 입학했다.\n이유: 14세에 Yale College에 다닐 \'준비를 하고 있었지만(was preparing)\', 옻나무 중독으로 시력이 손상되어 \'입학할 수 없었다(could not enter)\'.\n포인트 ▶ \'준비하고 있었다\'만 보고 입학했다고 착각하면 안 돼요. but 뒤를 꼭 확인!', per: [['14세에 Yale College에 입학했다.', 'but he could not enter due to a severe sumac poisoning', '불일치'], ['어렸을 때 아버지와 시골로 여행을 다녔다.', 'he often went on trips to the countryside with his father', '일치'], ['1858년 Central Park 설계 공모전 당선', 'won the design competition for New York City\'s Central Park', '일치'], ['Civil War 기간 중 Sanitary Commission에서 일함', 'Olmsted served as General Secretary of the United States Sanitary Commission', '일치'], ['집과 사무실이 National Historic Site로 지정', 'designated his home and office in Brookline', '일치']], n: 26 },
      { q: '<b>27. Ashwood Dunes Reforestation Event에 관한 다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하지 <u>않는</u> 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en"><b>Ashwood Dunes Reforestation Event</b><br>Join us for a meaningful morning at the annual Ashwood Dunes Reforestation. Volunteers will plant native tree seedlings to help restore the local environment. No special skills required, feel free to join us.<br><b>When and Where</b><br>• September 5th, 2026, 8 a.m.-11 a.m.<br>• Ashwood Dunes Wilderness<br><b>Participation Fee:</b> Free<br><b>Rewards:</b> All participants will get a commemorative badge.<br>• Plant 5 trees: Receive a reusable water bottle<br>• Plant 10 or more trees: Receive a T-shirt<br><b>Registration:</b> Online registration is encouraged, but on-site sign-ups will be available on the day of the event.<br><b>Event Guidelines</b><br>• Bring a hat and gloves; shovels and water will be prepared.<br>• Follow instructions from the event staff.<br>For more information, please visit www.ashwooddunes.com.</span>', opts: ['③ 나무 다섯 그루를 심으면 티셔츠를 받는다.', '① 매년 개최되는 행사이다.', '② 행사는 3시간 동안 진행된다.', '④ 당일 현장에서 등록할 수 있다.', '⑤ 모자와 장갑을 가져와야 한다.'], ex: '정답 ▶ ③ 나무 다섯 그루를 심으면 티셔츠를 받는다.\n이유: 5그루 → 재사용 물병(a reusable water bottle), 10그루 이상 → 티셔츠예요.\n나머지 ▶ ① annual = 매년 ✔ ② 8 a.m.-11 a.m. = 3시간 ✔ ④ on-site sign-ups ✔ ⑤ Bring a hat and gloves ✔\n포인트 ▶ 안내문은 숫자·조건(5 / 10 or more)과 보상을 짝지어 꼼꼼히 대조하세요.', per: [['나무 5그루 → 티셔츠', 'Plant 5 trees: Receive a reusable water bottle', '불일치 → 물병'], ['매년 개최', 'the annual Ashwood Dunes Reforestation', '일치'], ['3시간 동안 진행', '8 a.m.-11 a.m.', '일치'], ['당일 현장 등록 가능', 'on-site sign-ups will be available on the day of the event', '일치'], ['모자와 장갑을 가져와야 함', 'Bring a hat and gloves', '일치']], n: 27 }
    ] },
    { type: 'pick', title: '어휘선택', guide: '문맥에 맞는 낱말을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'When Olmsted was young, he often went on trips to the countryside with his father, who loved {scenery|scenario}.', ex: '정답 ▶ scenery (경치)\n오답 ▶ scenario (시나리오): 시골 여행을 다니던 아버지는 \'경치\'를 사랑했어요. → 조경가가 된 배경' },
      { s: 'At age 14, he was preparing himself to study at Yale College, but he could not enter due to a {severe|several} sumac poisoning that damaged his eyesight.', ex: '정답 ▶ severe (심각한)\n오답 ▶ several (몇몇의): 시력을 손상시킬 만큼 \'심각한\' 중독. a several ✘' },
      { s: 'Although he passed away in 1903, his sons and {successors|successes} continued his design philosophy.', ex: '정답 ▶ successors (후계자들)\n오답 ▶ successes (성공들): 아들들\'과 후계자들\'이 철학을 이어 갔다 → 사람' },
      { s: 'In 1979, the National Park Service {designated|delegated} his home and office in Brookline, Massachusetts, as a National Historic Site to honor his legacy.', ex: '정답 ▶ designated (지정했다)\n오답 ▶ delegated (위임했다): designate A as B = A를 B로 지정하다' },
      { s: 'Volunteers will plant native tree {seedlings|seasonings} to help restore the local environment.', ex: '정답 ▶ seedlings (묘목)\n오답 ▶ seasonings (양념): 숲 가꾸기 행사에서 심는 것 = 나무 \'묘목\'' },
      { s: 'Rewards: All participants will get a {commemorative|competitive} badge.', ex: '정답 ▶ commemorative (기념하는)\n오답 ▶ competitive (경쟁의): 참가자 모두에게 주는 \'기념\' 배지' },
      { s: 'Registration: Online registration is encouraged, but {on-site|online} sign-ups will be available on the day of the event.', ex: '정답 ▶ on-site (현장의)\n오답 ▶ online: \'but\'으로 온라인 등록과 대비 → 당일 \'현장\' 등록도 가능' }
    ] },
    { type: 'tf', title: 'T/F 내용 확인', guide: '본문 내용과 일치하면 T, 다르면 F를 고르세요.', items: [['Olmsted is regarded as the father of American landscape architecture.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: known as the father of American landscape architecture\n어휘 ▶ be regarded as = be known as', 26, 'known as the father of American landscape architecture'], ['Olmsted entered Yale College at the age of 14.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 준비하던 중 옻나무 중독으로 입학할 수 없었어요.', 26, 'but he could not enter'], ['Olmsted worked several jobs instead of going to college.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: 대학 대신 점원, 선원, 농부로 일했어요.', 26, 'Instead of attending a college, he gained experience working'], ['Olmsted\'s sons abandoned his design philosophy after his death.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 아들들과 후계자들이 그의 철학을 이어 갔어요(continued).\n어휘 ▶ abandon 버리다', 26, 'his sons and successors continued his design philosophy'], ['The event takes place every year.', true, '정답 ▶ T\n이유: annual = 매년 열리는', 27, 'the annual Ashwood Dunes Reforestation'], ['Participants need special gardening skills.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 특별한 기술은 필요 없어요(No special skills required).', 27, 'No special skills required'], ['Those who plant 5 trees will receive a T-shirt.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 5그루는 물병, 티셔츠는 10그루 이상이에요.', 27, 'Plant 5 trees: Receive a reusable water bottle'], ['Participants should bring their own shovels.', false, '정답 ▶ F\n이유: 삽과 물은 준비되어 있어요. 가져올 것은 모자와 장갑', 27, 'shovels and water will be prepared']] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '일치·불일치', guide: '선택지는 본문을 어려운 어휘로 바꿔 쓴 문장이에요. 풀고 나서 해설의 어휘를 꼭 확인하세요.', items: [
      { q: '글의 내용과 일치하지 <u>않는</u> 것은?', opts: ['He designed Central Park entirely on his own.', 'His father had a love of scenery.', 'An illness damaged his eyesight when he was young.', 'He held a position during the Civil War.', 'His home was named a National Historic Site in 1979.'], ex: '정답 ▶ He designed Central Park entirely on his own.\n이유: Calvert Vaux와 함께 작업했어요(worked with Calvert Vaux).\n어휘 ▶ entirely on one\'s own 완전히 혼자서 · hold a position 직책을 맡다', per: [['그는 Central Park를 완전히 혼자 설계했다.', 'he worked with Calvert Vaux', '불일치'], ['그의 아버지는 경치를 사랑했다.', 'his father, who loved scenery', '일치'], ['어릴 때 병으로 시력이 손상되었다.', 'a severe sumac poisoning that damaged his eyesight', '일치'], ['남북 전쟁 동안 직책을 맡았다.', 'During the Civil War, Olmsted served as General Secretary', '일치'], ['1979년 그의 집이 국가 사적지가 되었다.', 'In 1979, the National Park Service designated his home', '일치']], n: 26, box: ['26번 · 내용 불일치'] },
      { q: '안내문의 내용과 <u>일치하는</u> 것은?', opts: ['Participants can register at the event site on the day.', 'The event lasts all day long.', 'Participants must pay a small fee.', 'Only those who plant 10 trees get a badge.', 'Participants must bring their own water.'], ex: '정답 ▶ Participants can register at the event site on the day.\n이유: on-site sign-ups will be available on the day of the event\n어휘 ▶ register 등록하다 · last 계속되다', per: [['참가자는 당일 행사 현장에서 등록할 수 있다.', 'on-site sign-ups will be available on the day of the event', '일치'], ['행사는 하루 종일 진행된다.', '8 a.m.-11 a.m.', '불일치 → 3시간'], ['참가자는 소액의 참가비를 내야 한다.', 'Participation Fee: Free', '불일치 → 무료'], ['10그루를 심은 사람만 배지를 받는다.', 'All participants will get a commemorative badge', '불일치 → 모두'], ['참가자는 물을 직접 가져와야 한다.', 'shovels and water will be prepared', '불일치 → 준비됨']], n: 27, box: ['27번 · 안내문 불일치'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'pick', title: '어법선택', guide: '어법상 알맞은 것을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'Frederick Law Olmsted, {known|knowing} as the father of American landscape architecture, was born in 1822.', ex: '정답 ▶ known\n이유: Olmsted가 \'알려진\'(수동) → 과거분사 known. 콤마 사이의 삽입구 (= who was known as ~)' },
      { s: 'When Olmsted was young, he often went on trips to the countryside with his father, {who|which} loved scenery.', ex: '정답 ▶ who\n이유: 선행사 his father(사람) + 콤마 뒤 계속적 용법 → who\n오답 ▶ which는 사물' },
      { s: 'At age 14, he was preparing himself to study at Yale College, but he could not enter {due to|because} a severe sumac poisoning that damaged his eyesight.', ex: '정답 ▶ due to\n이유: 뒤에 명사(a severe sumac poisoning)가 오므로 전치사(구) due to\n오답 ▶ because 뒤에는 \'주어 + 동사\'' },
      { s: 'Instead of {attending|attend} a college, he gained experience working as a clerk, a sailor, and a farmer.', ex: '정답 ▶ attending\n이유: 전치사 of 뒤에는 동명사. instead of -ing = ~하는 대신에' },
      { s: '{Although|Despite} he passed away in 1903, his sons and successors continued his design philosophy.', ex: '정답 ▶ Although\n이유: 뒤에 \'주어 + 동사(he passed away)\'가 오므로 접속사 Although\n오답 ▶ Despite는 전치사 → 뒤에 명사(Despite his death)' },
      { s: 'Volunteers will plant native tree seedlings {to help|helping} restore the local environment.', ex: '정답 ▶ to help\n이유: \'~을 돕기 위해\'(목적) → to부정사. help + 동사원형(restore)' },
      { s: 'No special skills {required|requiring}, feel free to join us.', ex: '정답 ▶ required\n이유: 기술이 \'요구되는\'(수동) → 과거분사 (= No special skills are required)' },
      { s: 'Registration: Online registration is {encouraged|encouraging}, but on-site sign-ups will be available on the day of the event.', ex: '정답 ▶ encouraged\n이유: 온라인 등록이 \'권장되는\' 것 → 수동 is + p.p.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'verb', title: '동사변형', guide: '( ) 안의 동사를 알맞은 형태로 바꿔 쓰세요.', items: [
      { s: 'At age 14, he was ___ himself to study at Yale College, but he could not enter due to a severe sumac poisoning that damaged his eyesight.', v: 'prepare', a: ['preparing'], ex: '정답 ▶ preparing\n이유: was + -ing = 과거진행 (~하고 있었다). 준비 \'중\'이었지 입학한 게 아니에요!' },
      { s: 'In 1858, he worked with Calvert Vaux and ___ the design competition for New York City\'s Central Park with the "Greensward Plan."', v: 'win', a: ['won'], ex: '정답 ▶ won\n이유: worked와 and로 이어진 과거 병렬. win - won - won' },
      { s: 'In 1979, the National Park Service ___ his home and office in Brookline, Massachusetts, as a National Historic Site to honor his legacy.', v: 'designate', a: ['designated'], ex: '정답 ▶ designated\n이유: In 1979 → 과거형. designate A as B = A를 B로 지정하다' }
    ] },
    { type: 'error', title: '오류찾기', guide: '어법상 틀린 곳을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'At age 14, he was preparing [[him]] to study at Yale College, but he could not [[enter]] due to a severe sumac poisoning that [[damaged]] his eyesight.', w: 0, fix: 'himself', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ① him → himself\n이유: 주어 he와 목적어가 같은 사람 → 재귀대명사 himself (prepare oneself 준비하다)\n나머지 ▶ ② could + 동사원형 ✔ ③ damaged(that의 선행사 poisoning 단수, 과거) ✔' },
      { s: 'Instead of attending a college, he [[gained]] experience [[worked]] as a clerk, a [[sailor]], and a farmer.', w: 1, fix: 'working', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ② worked → working\n이유: gain experience -ing = ~하면서 경험을 쌓다. 문장의 동사는 gained 하나예요.\n나머지 ▶ ① gained(문장의 동사, 과거) ✔ ③ sailor(직업 명사 병렬) ✔' },
      { s: '[[Although]] he [[passed away]] in 1903, his sons and successors [[continuing]] his design philosophy.', w: 2, fix: 'continued', ex: '틀린 곳 ▶ ③ continuing → continued\n이유: 주절(his sons and successors ~)에 동사가 필요해요. 과거 → continued\n나머지 ▶ ① Although(접속사 + 절) ✔ ② passed away(세상을 떠났다) ✔' }
    ] },
    { type: 'scramble', title: '본문 스크램블', guide: '우리말에 맞게 단어를 눌러 문장을 완성하세요.', items: [
      { ko: '미국 조경 건축의 아버지로 알려진 Frederick Law Olmsted는 1822년에 태어났다.', en: 'Frederick Law Olmsted, known as the father of American landscape architecture, was born in 1822.' },
      { ko: '대학에 다니는 대신, 그는 점원, 선원, 농부로 일하며 경험을 쌓았다.', en: 'Instead of attending a college, he gained experience working as a clerk, a sailor, and a farmer.' },
      { ko: '남북 전쟁 동안 Olmsted는 미국 위생 위원회의 사무총장으로 일했다.', en: 'During the Civil War, Olmsted served as General Secretary of the United States Sanitary Commission.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'order', title: '문장순서', guide: '문장을 본문 순서대로 눌러서 배열하세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['Frederick Law Olmsted, known as the father of American landscape architecture, was born in 1822.', 'When Olmsted was young, he often went on trips to the countryside with his father, who loved scenery.', 'At age 14, he was preparing himself to study at Yale College, but he could not enter due to a severe sumac poisoning that damaged his eyesight.', 'Instead of attending a college, he gained experience working as a clerk, a sailor, and a farmer.'], flow: ['1822년 출생, 조경 건축의 아버지', '어릴 때 아버지와 시골 여행', '14세 Yale 준비 → 옻나무 중독으로 입학 못 함', '대학 대신 점원·선원·농부로 경험'] },
      { lines: ['In 1858, he worked with Calvert Vaux and won the design competition for New York City\'s Central Park with the "Greensward Plan."', 'During the Civil War, Olmsted served as General Secretary of the United States Sanitary Commission.', 'Although he passed away in 1903, his sons and successors continued his design philosophy.', 'In 1979, the National Park Service designated his home and office in Brookline, Massachusetts, as a National Historic Site to honor his legacy.'], flow: ['1858년 Central Park 공모전 당선', '남북 전쟁 중 위생 위원회 사무총장', '1903년 사망, 후계자들이 철학 계승', '1979년 집과 사무실이 국가 사적지'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'insert', title: '문장삽입', guide: '주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 알맞은 곳을 고르세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['At age 14, he was preparing himself to study at Yale College, but he could not enter due to a severe sumac poisoning that damaged his eyesight.', 'Instead of attending a college, he gained experience working as a clerk, a sailor, and a farmer.', 'In 1858, he worked with Calvert Vaux and won the design competition for New York City\'s Central Park with the "Greensward Plan."'], given: 1, flow: ['Yale에 들어갈 수 없었음', '대학에 다니는 대신 여러 직업으로 경험', '1858년 Central Park 공모전 당선'], ex: '정답 ▶ ①\n이유: Instead of attending a college(대학에 다니는 대신)는 \'입학할 수 없었다\' 바로 뒤에 와야 자연스러워요.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'odd', title: '어색한 문장', guide: '글의 흐름과 관계없는 문장을 고르세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['In 1858, he worked with Calvert Vaux and won the design competition for New York City\'s Central Park with the "Greensward Plan."', 'During the Civil War, Olmsted served as General Secretary of the United States Sanitary Commission.', 'Olmsted spent most of his life working as a sailor at sea.', 'Although he passed away in 1903, his sons and successors continued his design philosophy.'], odd: 2, ex: '정답 ▶ ③\n이유: 선원은 젊을 때 경험한 여러 직업 중 하나일 뿐이에요. 그는 조경가로 Central Park를 설계했어요.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'blank', title: '빈칸추론', guide: '빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'At age 14, he was preparing himself to study at Yale College, but he could not enter due to a severe sumac poisoning that damaged his ____.', opts: ['eyesight', 'hearing', 'legs', 'voice'], ex: '정답 ▶ eyesight\n이유: 옻나무 중독이 \'시력\'을 손상시켰어요. 그래서 공부(입학)를 할 수 없었어요.' },
      { s: 'Although he passed away in 1903, his sons and successors ____ his design philosophy.', opts: ['continued', 'abandoned', 'criticized', 'forgot'], ex: '정답 ▶ continued\n이유: Although(비록 ~지만)로 대비 → 그는 죽었지만 철학은 \'이어졌다\'' },
      { s: 'Rewards: All participants will get a commemorative ____.', opts: ['badge', 'T-shirt', 'water bottle', 'hat'], ex: '정답 ▶ badge\n이유: \'모든\' 참가자가 받는 것은 기념 배지. 물병(5그루)·티셔츠(10그루 이상)는 조건이 있어요.' },
      { s: 'Event Guidelines: Bring a hat and gloves; ____ and water will be prepared.', opts: ['shovels', 'hats', 'gloves', 'lunches'], ex: '정답 ▶ shovels\n이유: 모자와 장갑은 가져와야 하고, 준비되어 있는 것은 \'삽\'과 물이에요.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '핵심문법', guide: '알맞은 답을 고르세요.', items: [
      { n: 26, q: '밑줄 친 <b>who</b>가 가리키는 것은?<br><b>He often went on trips to the countryside with his father, <u>who</u> loved scenery.</b>', opts: ['his father', 'Olmsted', 'Calvert Vaux', 'his sons'], ex: '정답 ▶ his father\n이유: 콤마 뒤 who는 바로 앞의 사람 his father를 보충 설명해요. 경치를 사랑한 사람 = 아버지' },
      { n: 26, q: '다음 문장의 뜻으로 알맞은 것은?<br><b>The National Park Service designated his home as a National Historic Site.</b>', opts: ['국립공원관리청은 그의 집을 국가 사적지로 지정했다.', '국립공원관리청은 국가 사적지에 그의 집을 지었다.', '그의 집은 국립공원관리청의 사무실이 되었다.', '국립공원관리청은 그의 집을 팔았다.'], ex: '정답 ▶ 국립공원관리청은 그의 집을 국가 사적지로 지정했다.\n이유: designate A as B = A를 B로 지정하다' },
      { n: 26, q: '밑줄 친 부분의 뜻으로 알맞은 것은?<br><b>Although he <u>passed away</u> in 1903, his sons continued his philosophy.</b>', opts: ['세상을 떠났다', '지나갔다', '통과했다', '기절했다'], ex: '정답 ▶ 세상을 떠났다\n이유: pass away = die의 정중한 표현\n비교 ▶ pass out 기절하다 · pass by 지나가다' }
    ] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '요약문 완성', guide: '지문을 읽고 요약문의 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말을 고르세요.', items: [
      { n: 26, box: ['26번 · 내용 불일치'], q: '다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, 빈칸 (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것은?<br><b>Although Olmsted could not (A) ______ college, he became a famous landscape architect whose design philosophy was (B) ______ by his successors.</b>', opts: ['(A) attend – (B) continued', '(A) attend – (B) rejected', '(A) teach – (B) continued', '(A) teach – (B) rejected', '(A) leave – (B) forgotten'], ex: '정답 ▶ (A) attend – (B) continued\n(A) 옻나무 중독으로 대학에 \'다닐\' 수 없었음 → attend\n(B) 후계자들이 철학을 \'이어 감\' → continued\n오답 ▶ rejected(거부된)·forgotten(잊힌)은 반대예요.', ev: ['he could not enter', 'his sons and successors continued his design philosophy'] }
    ] }
  ] },
  { id: 'd5', name: 'DAY 5', range: '29·30번', story: '29 어법 · 30 어휘', passage: [['#', '29번 · 어법', ''], ['', 'Capitalism is not a system where cash is everywhere but rather one in which islands of cash exchange exist within oceans of cheap — or potentially cheap — natures.', '자본주의는 현금이 어디에나 존재하는 시스템이라기보다는, 값싼 혹은 잠재적으로 값싼 만물의 바다 안에 현금 교환이라는 섬들이 존재하는 시스템이다.'], ['', 'Reproducing life within the cash nexus is expensive, and it grows more expensive over time.', '현금 거래 관계 내에서 삶을 재생산하는 것은 비싸고, 시간이 흐를수록 그것은 더 비싸진다.'], ['', 'Workers\' wages can be frozen, even rolled back, but in the end inequality leads to crises of the kind we\'ve recently seen bring about populist protests in the United States and the United Kingdom.', '노동자의 임금은 동결되거나 심지어 삭감될 수 있지만, 결국 불평등은 우리가 최근 미국과 영국에서 포퓰리스트 시위를 발생시켰다고 목격한 것과 같은 종류의 위기를 초래한다.'], ['', 'Workers demand dignity, and their labor becomes expensive.', '노동자들은 존엄성을 요구하고, 그들의 노동력은 비싸진다.'], ['', 'Production processes burn through an island, and energy is no longer cheap.', '생산 과정은 섬(현금 교환의 섬)을 고갈시키며, 에너지는 더 이상 값싸지 않게 된다.'], ['', 'The climate changes, and crops can no longer grow as abundantly as they once did.', '기후가 변화하고, 작물은 한때 그랬던 것처럼 더 이상 풍부하게 자라지 않는다.'], ['', 'Frontiers are so important in these processes because they offer places where the new cheap things can be seized — and the cheap work of humans and other natures can be forcefully taken.', '이러한 과정에서 미개척 영역이 중요한데, 왜냐하면 그것이 새로운 값싼 것들이 확보될 수 있는 장소를 제공하기 때문이다. 즉, 값싼 인간의 노동력과 다른 만물이 강제로 취해질 수 있다는 것이다.'], ['#', '30번 · 어휘', ''], ['', 'Some people are more likely to cheat and steal after purchasing "eco-friendly" products, probably because they feel licensed to do so since they morally account the "eco-friendly" choice as a "good" deed.', '어떤 사람들은 \'친환경\' 제품을 구매한 후에 속이거나 훔칠 가능성이 더 높은데, 아마도 그들은 \'친환경적\' 선택을 \'착한\' 행위라고 도덕적으로 간주하여 그렇게 할 자격이 있다고 느끼기 때문일 것이다.'], ['', 'Moral licensing has also been observed in the context of cooperation for the good of the environment.', '도덕적 허가는 환경의 이익을 위한 협조의 맥락 안에서도 관찰되어 왔다.'], ['', 'When people are reminded of deeds they have done that they know were harmful to the environment, they might be left with a feeling of "eco-guilt."', '사람들이 자신이 해왔던 환경에 해롭다고 알고 있는 행동들을 떠올릴 때, 그들에게는 \'환경에 대한 죄책감\'이라는 감정이 남을 수 있다.'], ['', 'People who experience eco-guilt seek pro-environmental actions to compensate for this guilt and restore balance.', '환경에 대한 죄책감을 경험하는 사람들은 이 죄책감을 보상하고 균형을 회복하기 위한 친환경적인 행동을 추구한다.'], ['', 'In their search for such a balance, people are inclined to believe in "quick-fixes," because they want to re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily as possible.', '이러한 균형을 찾는 과정에서 사람들은 \'임시방편\'을 믿으려 하는 경향이 있는데, 이는 그들이 도덕적 균형을 재정립하고 가능한 한 빠르고 쉽게 죄책감을 벗어나고 싶어 하기 때문이다.'], ['', 'In the same way that people may change their attitudes to reduce inconsistencies between attitudes and behavior, people may also change their evaluation of past environmentally burdening behaviors and future environmentally friendly behaviors.', '사람들이 태도와 행동 사이의 불일치를 줄이기 위해 태도를 바꿀 수 있는 것과 같은 방식으로, 사람들은 과거의 환경에 부담을 주는 행동과 미래의 친환경적 행동에 대한 평가를 바꿀 수도 있다.'], ['', 'For instance, people are more likely to sign a petition addressing environmental damage after viewing evidence of human-caused environmental damage.', '예를 들어, 사람들은 인간이 유발한 환경 훼손의 증거를 본 후에 환경 훼손을 다루는 청원서에 서명할 가능성이 더 높다.']], steps: [
    { type: 'vocab', title: '핵심어휘·숙어', quizN: 15, items: [['capitalism', '자본주의', 29], ['potentially', '잠재적으로', 29], ['reproduce', '재생산하다', 29], ['wage', '임금', 29], ['freeze', '동결하다', 29], ['roll back', '인하하다, 되돌리다', 29], ['inequality', '불평등', 29], ['bring about', '초래하다', 29], ['populist', '대중 영합적인', 29], ['dignity', '존엄성', 29], ['burn through', '다 써 버리다', 29], ['abundantly', '풍부하게', 29], ['frontier', '미개척 영역, 변경', 29], ['seize', '빼앗다, 장악하다', 29], ['forcefully', '강제로', 29], ['eco-friendly', '친환경적인', 30], ['licensed', '허가받은, 자격이 있는', 30], ['account A as B', 'A를 B로 여기다', 30], ['deed', '행위', 30], ['moral licensing', '도덕적 허가', 30], ['observe', '관찰하다', 30], ['cooperation', '협력', 30], ['remind A of B', 'A에게 B를 떠올리게 하다', 30], ['guilt', '죄책감', 30], ['pro-environmental', '친환경적인', 30], ['compensate for', '~을 보상하다', 30], ['restore', '회복하다', 30], ['be inclined to', '~하는 경향이 있다', 30], ['reluctant', '꺼리는', 30], ['quick-fix', '임시방편', 30], ['re-establish', '재정립하다', 30], ['inconsistency', '불일치', 30], ['evaluation', '평가', 30], ['burdening', '부담을 주는', 30], ['petition', '청원서', 30], ['address', '다루다', 30]] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '실전 문항', guide: '시험지 원문항이에요. 지문을 읽고 답을 고르세요.', items: [
      { n: 29, q: '<b>29. 다음 글의 밑줄 친 부분 중, 어법상 <u>틀린</u> 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">Capitalism is not a system where cash is everywhere but rather ①<u>one</u> in which islands of cash exchange exist within oceans of cheap — or potentially cheap — natures. ②<u>Reproducing</u> life within the cash nexus is expensive, and it grows more expensive over time. Workers\' wages can be frozen, even rolled back, but in the end inequality leads to crises of the kind we\'ve recently seen ③<u>bring</u> about populist protests in the United States and the United Kingdom. Workers demand dignity, and their labor becomes expensive. Production processes burn through an island, and energy is no longer cheap. The climate changes, and crops can no longer grow as ④<u>abundantly</u> as they once did. Frontiers are so important in these processes because they offer places ⑤<u>which</u> the new cheap things can be seized — and the cheap work of humans and other natures can be forcefully taken.<br><small>*cash nexus: 현금 거래 관계</small></span>', opts: ['⑤ which', '① one', '② Reproducing', '③ bring', '④ abundantly'], ex: '정답 ⑤ which → where (in which). places 뒤에 완전한 절(the new cheap things can be seized)이 오므로 관계부사', per: [['', 'they offer places where the new cheap things can be seized', '뒤에 주어·동사가 다 갖춰진 완전한 절 → where / in which'], ['', 'but rather one in which islands of cash exchange exist', 'a system(단수 가산명사) 반복 → 부정대명사 one ✓'], ['', 'Reproducing life within the cash nexus is expensive', '주어 자리 동명사 → 단수동사 is ✓'], ['', 'we\'ve recently seen bring about populist protests', '지각동사 see + O(crises) + 동사원형 ✓ (O는 앞의 crises, 관계대명사 생략)'], ['', 'crops can no longer grow as abundantly as they once did', '동사 grow 수식 → 부사 abundantly ✓']] },
      { n: 30, q: '<b>30. 다음 글의 밑줄 친 부분 중, 문맥상 낱말의 쓰임이 적절하지 <u>않은</u> 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">Some people are more likely to cheat and steal after purchasing "eco-friendly" products, probably because they feel licensed to do so since they morally account the "eco-friendly" choice as a "good" deed. Moral licensing has also been ①<u>observed</u> in the context of cooperation for the good of the environment. When people are reminded of deeds they have done that they know were harmful to the environment, they might be left with a feeling of "eco-guilt." People who experience eco-guilt seek pro-environmental actions to compensate for this guilt and ②<u>restore</u> balance. In their search for such a balance, people are ③<u>reluctant</u> to believe in "quick-fixes," because they want to re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily as possible. In the same way that people may change their attitudes to reduce inconsistencies between attitudes and behavior, people may also change their ④<u>evaluation</u> of past environmentally burdening behaviors and future environmentally friendly behaviors. For instance, people are ⑤<u>more</u> likely to sign a petition addressing environmental damage after viewing evidence of human-caused environmental damage.<br><small>*petition: 청원서</small></span>', opts: ['③ reluctant', '① observed', '② restore', '④ evaluation', '⑤ more'], ex: '정답 ③ reluctant → inclined. 뒤에 \'가능한 한 빠르고 쉽게 죄책감에서 벗어나고 싶어서\'라는 이유가 오므로 임시방편을 믿으려 하는(inclined) 경향', per: [['꺼리는 → 하려는 경향이 있는', 'because they want to re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily as possible', '빨리 벗어나고 싶다 → 임시방편을 \'믿으려\' 함'], ['관찰된', 'Moral licensing has also been observed', '도덕적 허가가 환경 맥락에서도 관찰됨 ✓'], ['회복하다', 'to compensate for this guilt and restore balance', '죄책감을 보상하고 균형을 \'회복\' ✓'], ['평가', 'may also change their evaluation of past environmentally burdening behaviors', '태도를 바꾸듯 \'평가\'도 바꿈 ✓'], ['더 (가능성이 높은)', 'after viewing evidence of human-caused environmental damage', '훼손 증거를 보면 죄책감 → 청원 서명 가능성이 \'더\' 높음 ✓']] }
    ] },
    { type: 'pick', title: '어휘선택', guide: '문맥에 맞는 낱말을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'Workers\' wages can be frozen, even rolled back, but in the end {inequality|equality} leads to crises of the kind we\'ve recently seen bring about populist protests in the United States and the United Kingdom.', ex: '위기와 시위를 초래하는 것 → inequality 불평등' },
      { s: 'Workers demand {dignity|dignify}, and their labor becomes expensive.', ex: '명사 자리 dignity 존엄성 (dignify는 동사)' },
      { s: 'The climate changes, and crops can no longer grow as {abundantly|scarcely} as they once did.', ex: 'no longer와 함께 \'예전만큼 풍부하게\' → abundantly' },
      { s: 'Frontiers are so important in these processes because they offer places where the new cheap things can be {seized|ceased} — and the cheap work of humans and other natures can be forcefully taken.', ex: 'seize 빼앗다 vs cease 중단하다' },
      { s: 'Some people are more likely to cheat and steal after purchasing "eco-friendly" products, probably because they feel {licensed|limited} to do so since they morally account the "eco-friendly" choice as a "good" deed.', ex: '착한 일을 했으니 나쁜 일을 할 \'자격\'이 있다고 느낌 → licensed' },
      { s: 'Moral licensing has also been {observed|obscured} in the context of cooperation for the good of the environment.', ex: 'observe 관찰하다 vs obscure 가리다' },
      { s: 'People who experience eco-guilt seek pro-environmental actions to {compensate|complicate} for this guilt and restore balance.', ex: 'compensate for 보상하다' },
      { s: 'People who experience eco-guilt seek pro-environmental actions to compensate for this guilt and {restore|disturb} balance.', ex: '균형을 \'회복\' → restore ★30번 ② 적절' },
      { s: 'In their search for such a balance, people are {inclined|reluctant} to believe in "quick-fixes," because they want to re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily as possible.', ex: '빨리 벗어나고 싶음 → inclined ★30번 정답(reluctant는 틀림)' },
      { s: 'In the same way that people may change their attitudes to reduce {inconsistencies|consistencies} between attitudes and behavior, people may also change their evaluation of past environmentally burdening behaviors and future environmentally friendly behaviors.', ex: '태도를 바꾸는 이유 → 불일치(inconsistencies)를 줄이려고' }
    ] },
    { type: 'tf', title: 'T/F 내용 확인', guide: '본문 내용과 일치하면 T, 다르면 F를 고르세요.', items: [['Under capitalism, cash exchange occurs everywhere.', false, '현금이 어디에나 있는 체제가 아니라, 값싼 것들의 바다 속 현금 교환의 섬', 29, 'Capitalism is not a system where cash is everywhere'], ['Sustaining life within the cash nexus becomes costlier as time passes.', true, 'sustain ≒ reproduce · costlier = more expensive · as time passes = over time', 29, 'it grows more expensive over time'], ['Inequality eventually triggers crises that can provoke populist protests.', true, 'eventually = in the end · trigger = lead to · provoke = bring about', 29, 'in the end inequality leads to crises'], ['Frontiers matter little in capitalism.', false, 'matter little 별로 중요하지 않다 → 매우 중요(so important)', 29, 'Frontiers are so important in these processes'], ['Buying green products may make some people feel entitled to behave unethically.', true, 'green = eco-friendly · feel entitled to = feel licensed to · behave unethically ≒ cheat and steal', 30, 'they feel licensed to do so'], ['Recalling environmentally harmful deeds can leave people feeling guilty.', true, 'recall ≒ be reminded of · leave A feeling guilty = be left with a feeling of eco-guilt', 30, 'they might be left with a feeling of "eco-guilt."'], ['People with eco-guilt tend to avoid pro-environmental actions.', false, 'avoid 피하다 → 친환경 행동을 추구(seek)', 30, 'seek pro-environmental actions'], ['Viewing evidence of human-caused damage makes people less willing to sign petitions.', false, 'less willing → 서명할 가능성이 더 높음', 30, 'people are more likely to sign a petition']] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '일치·불일치', guide: '선택지는 본문을 어려운 어휘로 바꿔 쓴 문장이에요. 풀고 나서 해설의 어휘를 꼭 확인하세요.', items: [
      { n: 29, q: '<b>29. 글의 내용과 <u>일치하는</u> 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">Capitalism is not a system where cash is everywhere but rather one in which islands of cash exchange exist within oceans of cheap — or potentially cheap — natures. Reproducing life within the cash nexus is expensive, and it grows more expensive over time. Workers\' wages can be frozen, even rolled back, but in the end inequality leads to crises of the kind we\'ve recently seen bring about populist protests in the United States and the United Kingdom. Workers demand dignity, and their labor becomes expensive. Production processes burn through an island, and energy is no longer cheap. The climate changes, and crops can no longer grow as abundantly as they once did. Frontiers are so important in these processes because they offer places where the new cheap things can be seized — and the cheap work of humans and other natures can be forcefully taken.</span>', opts: ['Workers\' demand for dignity makes their labor more costly.', 'Capitalism is a system in which cash is present everywhere.', 'Workers\' wages can never be reduced under capitalism.', 'Crops now grow more abundantly than before.', 'Energy becomes cheaper as production processes continue.'], ex: '정답: demand for dignity 존엄성 요구 · costly = expensive\n· present everywhere → 어디에나 있는 체제가 아님(not a system where cash is everywhere)\n· never be reduced → 동결되거나 삭감될 수 있음(rolled back)\n· more abundantly → 더 이상 풍부하게 자라지 않음\n· cheaper → 더 이상 값싸지 않음(no longer cheap)', per: [['노동자의 존엄성 요구가 노동력을 더 비싸게 만든다.', 'Workers demand dignity, and their labor becomes expensive', '일치'], ['자본주의는 현금이 어디에나 존재하는 체제이다.', 'Capitalism is not a system where cash is everywhere', '불일치'], ['자본주의에서 노동자의 임금은 결코 깎일 수 없다.', 'can be frozen, even rolled back', '불일치'], ['작물이 지금 예전보다 더 풍부하게 자란다.', 'crops can no longer grow as abundantly as they once did', '불일치'], ['생산 과정이 계속되며 에너지가 더 싸진다.', 'energy is no longer cheap', '불일치']] },
      { n: 30, q: '<b>30. 글의 내용과 일치하지 <u>않는</u> 것은?</b><span class="exam-box en">Some people are more likely to cheat and steal after purchasing "eco-friendly" products, probably because they feel licensed to do so since they morally account the "eco-friendly" choice as a "good" deed. Moral licensing has also been observed in the context of cooperation for the good of the environment. When people are reminded of deeds they have done that they know were harmful to the environment, they might be left with a feeling of "eco-guilt." People who experience eco-guilt seek pro-environmental actions to compensate for this guilt and restore balance. In their search for such a balance, people are inclined to believe in "quick-fixes," because they want to re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily as possible. In the same way that people may change their attitudes to reduce inconsistencies between attitudes and behavior, people may also change their evaluation of past environmentally burdening behaviors and future environmentally friendly behaviors. For instance, people are more likely to sign a petition addressing environmental damage after viewing evidence of human-caused environmental damage.</span>', opts: ['People with eco-guilt are unwilling to take quick measures to ease their guilt.', 'Some people regard eco-friendly purchases as morally good deeds.', 'Moral licensing appears in environmental cooperation as well.', 'People may reassess their past behaviors that burdened the environment.', 'Evidence of environmental harm can prompt people to sign petitions.'], ex: '정답: unwilling = reluctant · quick measures = quick-fixes → 실제로는 임시방편을 믿으려 함(inclined)\n· regard A as B = account A as B\n· as well = also\n· reassess = change one\'s evaluation · burden 부담을 주다\n· prompt A to ~ A가 ~하도록 자극하다', per: [['환경 죄책감을 느끼는 사람들은 죄책감을 덜기 위한 빠른 조치를 꺼린다.', 'people are inclined to believe in "quick-fixes,"', '불일치 → 오히려 믿으려 함'], ['어떤 사람들은 친환경 구매를 도덕적으로 좋은 행위로 여긴다.', 'they morally account the "eco-friendly" choice as a "good" deed', '일치'], ['도덕적 허가는 환경 협력에서도 나타난다.', 'Moral licensing has also been observed in the context of cooperation', '일치'], ['사람들은 환경에 부담을 준 과거 행동을 재평가할 수 있다.', 'change their evaluation of past environmentally burdening behaviors', '일치'], ['환경 피해의 증거는 사람들이 청원에 서명하게 할 수 있다.', 'more likely to sign a petition addressing environmental damage', '일치']] }
    ] },
    { type: 'pick', title: '어법선택', guide: '어법상 알맞은 것을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'Capitalism is not a system where cash is everywhere but rather {one|it} in which islands of cash exchange exist within oceans of cheap — or potentially cheap — natures.', ex: 'a system 반복 + 뒤에 수식어 → 부정대명사 one' },
      { s: '{Reproducing|Reproduce} life within the cash nexus is expensive, and it grows more expensive over time.', ex: '주어 자리 → 동명사' },
      { s: 'Workers\' wages can be frozen, even rolled back, but in the end inequality leads to crises of the kind we\'ve recently seen {bring|to bring} about populist protests in the United States and the United Kingdom.', ex: '지각동사 see + O + 동사원형' },
      { s: 'The climate changes, and crops can no longer grow as {abundantly|abundant} as they once did.', ex: '동사 grow 수식 → 부사' },
      { s: 'Frontiers are so important in these processes because they offer places {where|which} the new cheap things can be seized — and the cheap work of humans and other natures can be forcefully taken.', ex: '뒤에 완전한 절 → 관계부사 where ★29번 정답' },
      { s: 'Frontiers are so important in these processes because they offer places where the new cheap things can be seized — and the cheap work of humans and {other|another} natures can be forcefully taken.', ex: '복수명사 natures 앞 → other' },
      { s: 'Some people are more likely to cheat and steal after {purchasing|purchase} "eco-friendly" products, probably because they feel licensed to do so since they morally account the "eco-friendly" choice as a "good" deed.', ex: '전치사 after + 동명사' },
      { s: 'Moral licensing has also {been observed|observed} in the context of cooperation for the good of the environment.', ex: '허가가 \'관찰된\' 수동 → has been observed' },
      { s: 'When people {are reminded|remind} of deeds they have done that they know were harmful to the environment, they might be left with a feeling of "eco-guilt."', ex: 'remind A of B의 수동 → be reminded of' },
      { s: 'When people are reminded of deeds they have done that they know {were|was} harmful to the environment, they might be left with a feeling of "eco-guilt."', ex: '선행사 deeds(복수) + 삽입절 they know → were' },
      { s: 'People who experience eco-guilt seek pro-environmental actions {to compensate|compensating} for this guilt and restore balance.', ex: '목적의 to부정사' },
      { s: 'In their search for such a balance, people are inclined to believe in "quick-fixes," because they want to re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily {as|than} possible.', ex: 'as ~ as possible' },
      { s: 'For instance, people are more likely to sign a petition {addressing|addressed} environmental damage after viewing evidence of human-caused environmental damage.', ex: '청원서가 피해를 \'다루는\' 능동 → addressing' }
    ] },
    { type: 'verb', title: '동사변형', guide: '( ) 안의 동사를 알맞은 형태로 바꿔 쓰세요.', items: [
      { s: '___ life within the cash nexus is expensive, and it grows more expensive over time.', v: 'reproduce', a: ['Reproducing', 'To reproduce'], ex: '주어 자리 동명사(to부정사)' },
      { s: 'Workers\' wages can be ___, even rolled back, but in the end inequality leads to crises of the kind we\'ve recently seen bring about populist protests in the United States and the United Kingdom.', v: 'freeze', a: ['frozen'], ex: 'freeze - froze - frozen, 수동태' },
      { s: 'Workers\' wages can be frozen, even rolled back, but in the end inequality leads to crises of the kind we\'ve recently ___ bring about populist protests in the United States and the United Kingdom.', v: 'see', a: ['seen'], ex: '현재완료 have p.p.' },
      { s: 'Frontiers are so important in these processes because they offer places where the new cheap things can be ___ — and the cheap work of humans and other natures can be forcefully taken.', v: 'seize', a: ['seized'], ex: '조동사 + be + p.p.' },
      { s: 'Some people are more likely to cheat and steal after purchasing "eco-friendly" products, probably because they feel ___ to do so since they morally account the "eco-friendly" choice as a "good" deed.', v: 'license', a: ['licensed'], ex: 'feel + 과거분사(자격을 받은)' },
      { s: 'When people are reminded of deeds they have done that they know were harmful to the environment, they might be ___ with a feeling of "eco-guilt."', v: 'leave', a: ['left'], ex: 'be left with ~이 남다' },
      { s: 'In their search for such a balance, people are ___ to believe in "quick-fixes," because they want to re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily as possible.', v: 'incline', a: ['inclined'], ex: 'be inclined to' },
      { s: 'For instance, people are more likely to sign a petition addressing environmental damage after ___ evidence of human-caused environmental damage.', v: 'view', a: ['viewing'], ex: 'after + 동명사' }
    ] },
    { type: 'error', title: '오류찾기', guide: '어법상 틀린 곳을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'Capitalism is not a system [[where]] cash is everywhere but rather [[it]] in which islands of cash exchange [[exist]] within oceans of [[cheap]] — or potentially cheap — natures.', w: 1, fix: 'one', ex: '수식어가 붙는 대명사 → one' },
      { s: '[[Reproducing]] life within the cash nexus [[are]] expensive, and it grows more [[expensive]] over time. Workers\' wages can be [[frozen]], even rolled back.', w: 1, fix: 'is', ex: '동명사 주어 → 단수동사' },
      { s: 'The climate changes, and crops can no longer grow as [[abundantly]] as they once [[did]]. Frontiers are so important in these processes because they offer places [[which]] the new cheap things can be [[seized]].', w: 2, fix: 'where', ex: '뒤에 완전한 절 → where' },
      { s: 'Moral licensing has also [[observed]] in the context of cooperation for the good of the environment. When people [[are reminded]] of deeds they have done that they know [[were]] harmful to the environment, they might be [[left]] with a feeling of "eco-guilt."', w: 0, fix: 'been observed', ex: '수동 완료 has been observed' },
      { s: 'People who [[experience]] eco-guilt [[seeks]] pro-environmental actions [[to compensate]] for this guilt and [[restore]] balance.', w: 1, fix: 'seek', ex: '주어 People(복수) → seek' },
      { s: 'For instance, people are more likely [[signing]] a petition [[addressing]] environmental damage after [[viewing]] evidence of [[human-caused]] environmental damage.', w: 0, fix: 'to sign', ex: 'be likely to + 동사원형' }
    ] },
    { type: 'scramble', title: '본문 스크램블', guide: '우리말에 맞게 단어를 눌러 문장을 완성하세요.', items: [
      { ko: '현금 거래 관계 내에서 삶을 재생산하는 것은 비싸고, 시간이 흐를수록 그것은 더 비싸진다.', en: 'Reproducing life within the cash nexus is expensive, and it grows more expensive over time.' },
      { ko: '노동자들은 존엄성을 요구하고, 그들의 노동력은 비싸진다.', en: 'Workers demand dignity, and their labor becomes expensive.' },
      { ko: '기후가 변화하고, 작물은 한때 그랬던 것처럼 더 이상 풍부하게 자라지 않는다.', en: 'The climate changes, and crops can no longer grow as abundantly as they once did.' },
      { ko: '도덕적 허가는 환경의 이익을 위한 협조의 맥락 안에서도 관찰되어 왔다.', en: 'Moral licensing has also been observed in the context of cooperation for the good of the environment.' },
      { ko: '환경에 대한 죄책감을 경험하는 사람들은 이 죄책감을 보상하고 균형을 회복하기 위한 친환경적인 행동을 추구한다.', en: 'People who experience eco-guilt seek pro-environmental actions to compensate for this guilt and restore balance.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'order', title: '문장순서', guide: '문장을 본문 순서대로 눌러서 배열하세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['Capitalism is not a system where cash is everywhere but rather one in which islands of cash exchange exist within oceans of cheap — or potentially cheap — natures.', 'Reproducing life within the cash nexus is expensive, and it grows more expensive over time.', 'Workers\' wages can be frozen, even rolled back, but in the end inequality leads to crises of the kind we\'ve recently seen bring about populist protests in the United States and the United Kingdom.', 'Workers demand dignity, and their labor becomes expensive.'], flow: ['자본주의 = 값싼 것들의 바다 속 현금 교환의 섬', '현금 관계 속 삶의 재생산은 점점 비싸짐', '임금을 묶어도 결국 불평등이 위기·시위 초래', '노동자가 존엄성 요구 → 노동력 비싸짐'] },
      { lines: ['Production processes burn through an island, and energy is no longer cheap.', 'The climate changes, and crops can no longer grow as abundantly as they once did.', 'Frontiers are so important in these processes because they offer places where the new cheap things can be seized — and the cheap work of humans and other natures can be forcefully taken.'], flow: ['생산이 섬을 소진 → 에너지 비싸짐', '기후 변화 → 작물 감소', '그래서 새 값싼 것을 빼앗을 미개척 영역이 중요'] },
      { lines: ['Some people are more likely to cheat and steal after purchasing "eco-friendly" products, probably because they feel licensed to do so since they morally account the "eco-friendly" choice as a "good" deed.', 'Moral licensing has also been observed in the context of cooperation for the good of the environment.', 'When people are reminded of deeds they have done that they know were harmful to the environment, they might be left with a feeling of "eco-guilt."', 'People who experience eco-guilt seek pro-environmental actions to compensate for this guilt and restore balance.'], flow: ['친환경 구매 후 \'착한 일 했으니\' 속이고 훔치기도 함', '도덕적 허가는 환경 협력에서도 관찰됨', '환경에 해로운 행동을 떠올리면 환경 죄책감', '죄책감을 보상하려 친환경 행동 추구'] },
      { lines: ['In their search for such a balance, people are inclined to believe in "quick-fixes," because they want to re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily as possible.', 'In the same way that people may change their attitudes to reduce inconsistencies between attitudes and behavior, people may also change their evaluation of past environmentally burdening behaviors and future environmentally friendly behaviors.', 'For instance, people are more likely to sign a petition addressing environmental damage after viewing evidence of human-caused environmental damage.'], flow: ['빨리 벗어나려 임시방편을 믿으려 함', '태도처럼 행동에 대한 평가도 바꿈', '예: 환경 피해 증거를 보면 청원 서명↑'] }
    ] },
    { type: 'insert', title: '문장삽입', guide: '주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 알맞은 곳을 고르세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['Workers\' wages can be frozen, even rolled back, but in the end inequality leads to crises of the kind we\'ve recently seen bring about populist protests in the United States and the United Kingdom.', 'Workers demand dignity, and their labor becomes expensive.', 'Production processes burn through an island, and energy is no longer cheap.', 'The climate changes, and crops can no longer grow as abundantly as they once did.'], given: 1, flow: ['불평등이 위기 초래', '노동력이 비싸짐', '에너지가 비싸짐', '작물이 줄어듦'], ex: '값싼 것들이 하나씩 비싸지는 흐름: 노동 → 에너지 → 작물' },
      { lines: ['When people are reminded of deeds they have done that they know were harmful to the environment, they might be left with a feeling of "eco-guilt."', 'People who experience eco-guilt seek pro-environmental actions to compensate for this guilt and restore balance.', 'In their search for such a balance, people are inclined to believe in "quick-fixes," because they want to re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily as possible.'], given: 1, flow: ['환경 죄책감이 남음', '이 죄책감(this guilt)을 보상하려 함', '그런 균형(such a balance)을 찾는 과정'], ex: 'this guilt는 eco-guilt, such a balance는 앞의 restore balance를 받는다.' }
    ] },
    { type: 'odd', title: '어색한 문장', guide: '글의 흐름과 관계없는 문장을 고르세요.', items: [
      { lines: ['Workers demand dignity, and their labor becomes expensive.', 'Production processes burn through an island, and energy is no longer cheap.', 'Some economists argue that cash will soon be replaced by digital currency.', 'The climate changes, and crops can no longer grow as abundantly as they once did.'], odd: 2, ex: '디지털 화폐 → 값싼 것들이 비싸지는 흐름과 관계없음' },
      { lines: ['When people are reminded of deeds they have done that they know were harmful to the environment, they might be left with a feeling of "eco-guilt."', 'People who experience eco-guilt seek pro-environmental actions to compensate for this guilt and restore balance.', 'Recycling programs have been adopted by many cities worldwide.', 'In their search for such a balance, people are inclined to believe in "quick-fixes," because they want to re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily as possible.'], odd: 2, ex: '재활용 프로그램 확산 → 죄책감과 균형 이야기와 관계없음' }
    ] },
    { type: 'blank', title: '빈칸추론', guide: '빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 알맞은 것을 고르세요.', items: [
      { s: 'Frontiers are so important in these processes because they offer places where ____.', opts: ['the new cheap things can be seized', 'cash is exchanged fairly', 'workers\' wages are raised', 'the climate is protected'], ex: '값싼 것들이 사라지므로 새로 빼앗을 곳이 필요' },
      { s: 'In their search for such a balance, people are inclined to believe in "quick-fixes," because they want to ____.', opts: ['re-establish the moral balance and escape the guilt as quickly and easily as possible', 'take time to solve environmental problems completely', 'avoid thinking about the environment', 'buy more eco-friendly products'], ex: '임시방편을 믿는 이유: 빨리, 쉽게 벗어나려고' },
      { s: 'For instance, people are more likely to sign a petition addressing environmental damage after ____.', opts: ['viewing evidence of human-caused environmental damage', 'buying eco-friendly products', 'forgetting their past behaviors', 'receiving money from charities'], ex: '죄책감을 일으키는 계기 → 피해 증거를 봄' }
    ] },
    { type: 'mc', title: '핵심문법', guide: '알맞은 답을 고르세요.', items: [
      { q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>Capitalism is not a system where cash is everywhere but rather ______ in which islands exist.</b>', opts: ['one', 'it', 'them', 'ones'], ex: '단수 가산명사 반복 + 수식어 → one' },
      { q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>crises of the kind we\'ve seen ______ about protests</b>', opts: ['bring', 'to bring', 'brought', 'brings'], ex: '지각동사 see + O + 동사원형' },
      { q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>Crops can no longer grow as ______ as they once did.</b>', opts: ['abundantly', 'abundant', 'abundance', 'abound'], ex: '동사 수식 → 부사' },
      { q: '빈칸에 들어갈 수 <u>없는</u> 것은?<br><b>They offer places ______ the new cheap things can be seized.</b>', opts: ['which', 'where', 'in which', 'that'], ex: '완전한 절 앞 → where / in which / (관계부사 대용) that 가능, which는 불가' },
      { q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>Moral licensing has also ______ in that context.</b>', opts: ['been observed', 'observed', 'being observed', 'to be observed'], ex: '현재완료 수동태' },
      { q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>When people are reminded ______ deeds they have done</b>', opts: ['of', 'to', 'with', 'for'], ex: 'remind A of B → be reminded of B' },
      { q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>deeds they have done that they know ______ harmful</b>', opts: ['were', 'was', 'being', 'to be'], ex: '선행사 deeds(복수) + 삽입절' },
      { q: '빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br><b>Escape the guilt as quickly ______ possible.</b>', opts: ['as', 'than', 'so', 'that'], ex: 'as ~ as possible' }
    ] }
  ] }
  ]
